// --- Lesson 1: The Nine Provinces of South Africa ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The Nine Provinces of South Africa\n\nSouth Africa is divided into **nine provinces**. A province is a large area of the country with its own government. Each province has a **premier** (leader) and its own capital city.\n\nHere are the nine provinces:\n\n| Province | Capital City |\n|----------|-------------|\n| **Gauteng** | Johannesburg (largest city) |\n| **Western Cape** | Cape Town |\n| **Eastern Cape** | Bhisho |\n| **KwaZulu-Natal** | Pietermaritzburg |\n| **Free State** | Bloemfontein |\n| **Limpopo** | Polokwane |\n| **Mpumalanga** | Mbombela (Nelspruit) |\n| **North West** | Mahikeng |\n| **Northern Cape** | Kimberley |\n\nSouth Africa also has **three capital cities** for the whole country:\n- **Pretoria** — where the President works (executive capital)\n- **Cape Town** — where Parliament meets (legislative capital)\n- **Bloemfontein** — where the highest court sits (judicial capital)'),
  q(2, 'How many provinces does South Africa have?',
    ['Nine provinces', 'Seven provinces', 'Eleven provinces', 'Five provinces'], 0,
    'South Africa has exactly nine provinces. They were created in 1994 when South Africa became a democracy. Before that, the country was divided differently during the apartheid era.',
    ['Think about how many players are in a netball team.']),
  t(3, '## Getting to Know Gauteng\n\nLet us look at **Gauteng** as an example of a province.\n\n### Fast Facts About Gauteng\n\n| Fact | Detail |\n|------|--------|\n| **Name meaning** | "Place of gold" in Sesotho |\n| **Size** | The smallest province in area, but the most people live here |\n| **Population** | Over 15 million people |\n| **Major cities** | Johannesburg, Pretoria, Ekurhuleni (East Rand) |\n| **Languages** | Zulu, English, Afrikaans, Sesotho, Setswana, and many others |\n| **Famous for** | Gold mining, business, the Cradle of Humankind, the Union Buildings |\n\n### History of Gauteng\n\nGauteng\'s history is closely linked to **gold**. In **1886**, gold was discovered on the Witwatersrand ridge. Thousands of people rushed to the area to find gold, and the city of **Johannesburg** grew very quickly. It went from a small mining camp to a huge city in just a few years.\n\nBefore the gold rush, the area was home to **Tswana, Sotho, and Ndebele** communities who had been farming and trading there for hundreds of years.'),
  fb(4, 'Gauteng means "place of ___" in Sesotho. Gold was discovered on the Witwatersrand in the year ___.',
    ['gold', '1886'],
    'The name Gauteng comes from the Sesotho word for gold. The discovery of gold in 1886 changed the area forever, turning it from farmland into the biggest city in South Africa — Johannesburg.'),
  t(5, '## Important Places in Gauteng\n\nGauteng has many important places that tell us about our history and culture:\n\n### The Cradle of Humankind\n\nThis is a **UNESCO World Heritage Site** near Johannesburg. Fossils of some of the **oldest human ancestors** in the world were found here. The most famous is **"Mrs Ples"**, a skull that is about 2.1 million years old! This place shows us that **Africa is the birthplace of all humans**.\n\n### The Union Buildings in Pretoria\n\nThis beautiful building is where the **President of South Africa** works. It was also where **Nelson Mandela** was inaugurated (made president) on **10 May 1994** — the first democratic president of South Africa.\n\n### The Apartheid Museum\n\nThis museum in Johannesburg tells the story of **apartheid** — the unfair system that separated people by race. It helps us understand our past so we can build a better future.\n\n### Constitution Hill\n\nThis is where the **Constitutional Court** stands today. It used to be a prison where many famous South Africans were kept, including **Nelson Mandela** and **Mahatma Gandhi**.'),
  q(6, 'Why is the Cradle of Humankind an important place?',
    ['Fossils of some of the oldest human ancestors in the world were found there', 'It is where gold was first discovered', 'It is where Nelson Mandela was born', 'It is the biggest shopping centre in Africa'], 0,
    'The Cradle of Humankind is a UNESCO World Heritage Site near Johannesburg. Scientists found fossils there — including "Mrs Ples" — that are millions of years old. These discoveries show that Africa is where the very first humans came from.',
    ['Think about what "cradle" means — the place where something begins.']),
  t(7, '## Important People from Gauteng\n\nMany famous South Africans have connections to Gauteng:\n\n- **Nelson Mandela** — Lived and worked in Johannesburg. He was a lawyer in the city before he fought against apartheid. He was imprisoned on Robben Island but later released and became our first democratic president.\n- **Albertina Sisulu** — A nurse and anti-apartheid activist from Soweto. She was called the "Mother of the Nation."\n- **Archbishop Desmond Tutu** — Lived in Soweto and won the **Nobel Peace Prize** for his fight against apartheid without using violence.\n- **Hector Pieterson** — A young boy who was shot during the **Soweto Uprising of 16 June 1976** when students protested against being forced to learn in Afrikaans. Youth Day on 16 June honours him and all the students.'),
  fb(8, 'Albertina Sisulu was known as the "Mother of the ___". The Soweto Uprising took place on 16 June ___.',
    ['Nation', '1976'],
    'Albertina Sisulu spent her life fighting for freedom and justice in South Africa, especially from Soweto. The Soweto Uprising of 16 June 1976 was a turning point in the struggle against apartheid, when students bravely protested against unfair education policies.'),
  q(9, 'What event does Youth Day on 16 June remember?',
    ['The Soweto Uprising of 1976 when students protested against being taught in Afrikaans', 'The day Nelson Mandela was released from prison', 'The day South Africa won the Rugby World Cup', 'The day gold was discovered in Johannesburg'], 0,
    'On 16 June 1976, thousands of school children in Soweto marched peacefully to protest against being forced to learn in Afrikaans. The police fired on the students and many were killed, including Hector Pieterson. Youth Day honours their bravery.'),
  t(10, '## Every Province Is Special\n\nEvery province in South Africa has its own special history, important places, and famous people. Here are some highlights from other provinces:\n\n| Province | Something Special |\n|----------|------------------|\n| **Western Cape** | Table Mountain, Robben Island, the oldest city (Cape Town) |\n| **KwaZulu-Natal** | The Zulu Kingdom, the Drakensberg mountains, Durban beaches |\n| **Eastern Cape** | Birthplace of Nelson Mandela (Mvezo), the Wild Coast |\n| **Free State** | Wide open farmlands, the Golden Gate Highlands National Park |\n| **Limpopo** | The Kruger National Park, Mapungubwe — an ancient African kingdom |\n| **Mpumalanga** | The Blyde River Canyon, God\'s Window, waterfalls |\n| **North West** | Sun City, Pilanesberg National Park, platinum mining |\n| **Northern Cape** | The biggest province by area, the Kalahari Desert, diamonds |\n\nYou should be proud of your province and know its history. Ask your family members and elders about the special places and stories from where you live!'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: The History of Our Province ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Learning About Our Province\'s Past\n\nEvery province in South Africa has a long and rich history. To understand our province, we need to learn about:\n- **Who lived there first** — the first people to call it home\n- **What happened over time** — important events that shaped the province\n- **Who were important people** — leaders, heroes, and ordinary people who made a difference\n\n### The First People\n\nLong before the provinces existed, different groups of people lived in different parts of South Africa:\n\n| Area | Early Peoples |\n|------|---------------|\n| **Western Cape, Northern Cape** | San hunter-gatherers and Khoikhoi herders |\n| **Gauteng, North West** | Tswana, Sotho, and Ndebele farming communities |\n| **KwaZulu-Natal** | Zulu and other Nguni peoples |\n| **Eastern Cape** | Xhosa people |\n| **Limpopo** | Venda, Tsonga, and Northern Sotho peoples |\n\nThese communities had their own languages, customs, and ways of governing. They farmed, kept cattle, traded with each other, and had rich cultural traditions.'),
  q(2, 'Why is it important to learn about the history of our province?',
    ['To understand who lived there first, what happened over time, and who the important people were', 'Because it is only in our textbook', 'To know how to drive to other provinces', 'Because our teacher told us to'], 0,
    'Learning about our province helps us understand where we come from and why things are the way they are today. It connects us to the people who lived before us and helps us appreciate our heritage.'),
  t(3, '## How Provinces Changed Over Time\n\nSouth Africa\'s provinces have not always looked the way they do today. Over hundreds of years, many things happened that changed who lived where:\n\n### 1. Early Communities\n\nFor thousands of years, different African communities lived, farmed, and traded across southern Africa. They built villages, kept cattle, grew crops, and had their own chiefs and kings.\n\n### 2. European Settlers Arrive\n\nFrom the **1600s**, European settlers began arriving. The Dutch came first in **1652**, starting at the Cape. Later, the British came too. Settlers took land from African communities and set up farms, towns, and mines.\n\n### 3. The Mineral Revolution\n\nThe discovery of **diamonds** near Kimberley in **1867** and **gold** on the Witwatersrand in **1886** changed South Africa forever. People from all over the world came to work in the mines. Cities grew rapidly. Railways were built to connect the mines to the ports.\n\n### 4. Apartheid (1948–1994)\n\nDuring apartheid, the government separated people by race. Black South Africans were forced to live in certain areas and could not move freely. Many people were removed from their homes.\n\n### 5. Democracy (1994–today)\n\nIn **1994**, South Africa had its first democratic election. The nine provinces were created, and all South Africans got the right to vote.'),
  fb(4, 'Diamonds were discovered near Kimberley in ___. South Africa\'s first democratic election was held in ___.',
    ['1867', '1994'],
    'The discovery of diamonds in 1867 started a "diamond rush" to Kimberley, which changed South Africa\'s economy. In 1994, all South Africans — regardless of race — voted for the first time in a free election, marking the end of apartheid.'),
  t(5, '## Researching Your Province\n\nThere are many ways to learn about the history of your province. Here are some ideas:\n\n### Talk to People\n- Ask your **grandparents** and **elders** about the past — they have amazing stories!\n- Interview **community members** who remember important events\n- Listen to **oral histories** — stories that are passed down by word of mouth\n\n### Visit Places\n- **Museums** — many towns have museums that show local history\n- **Heritage sites** — places where important events happened\n- **Libraries** — they have books, photos, and old newspapers about your area\n\n### Look at Sources\n- **Old photographs** — show you what places looked like long ago\n- **Maps** — old maps show how boundaries and towns changed\n- **Newspapers** — old newspaper articles tell about events that happened\n- **Objects** (artefacts) — old tools, clothes, and other objects tell stories about how people lived'),
  q(6, 'Which of these is a good way to learn about the history of your province?',
    ['Talking to grandparents and elders, visiting museums, and looking at old photographs', 'Only reading your textbook and nothing else', 'Watching cartoons on television', 'Making up stories about what you think happened'], 0,
    'There are many ways to learn about local history. Talking to older family members and community elders is especially valuable because they can share stories that are not written in books. Museums, heritage sites, old photos, and libraries are also excellent sources.',
    ['Think about where you might find information about the past.']),
  t(7, '## Important Events in South African Provinces\n\nEvery province has events in its past that shaped what it is today. Here are some important events from around the country:\n\n| Province | Important Event |\n|----------|----------------|\n| **Gauteng** | Soweto Uprising (1976) — students protested against apartheid education |\n| **Western Cape** | Founding of Cape Town (1652) — first European settlement |\n| **KwaZulu-Natal** | Battle of Isandlwana (1879) — Zulu warriors defeated the British army |\n| **Eastern Cape** | The Frontier Wars — conflicts between Xhosa people and British settlers |\n| **Free State** | The Anglo-Boer War (1899–1902) — major battles were fought here |\n| **Limpopo** | Kingdom of Mapungubwe — a powerful African trading kingdom (around 1075–1220 CE) |\n| **Northern Cape** | Diamond rush at Kimberley (1867) — changed South Africa\'s economy |\n| **Mpumalanga** | The old Transvaal gold rush — mining towns like Barberton |\n| **North West** | Sun City was built in 1979 in what was then "Bophuthatswana" — one of the apartheid homelands |'),
  fb(8, 'In 1879, Zulu warriors defeated the British army at the Battle of ___. The ancient African kingdom of ___ was in Limpopo.',
    ['Isandlwana', 'Mapungubwe'],
    'The Battle of Isandlwana in 1879 was one of the greatest defeats of the British army anywhere in the world. Mapungubwe was a powerful African kingdom that traded gold and ivory with people as far away as China and India. It shows that Africa had advanced civilisations long before Europeans arrived.'),
  q(9, 'What was Mapungubwe?',
    ['A powerful ancient African trading kingdom in Limpopo', 'A type of food eaten in the Northern Cape', 'A mountain in the Western Cape', 'A river in Mpumalanga'], 0,
    'Mapungubwe was an advanced African kingdom in what is now Limpopo province. It existed from about 1075 to 1220 CE. The people of Mapungubwe traded gold, ivory, and other goods with traders from East Africa, India, and China. A famous golden rhinoceros was found there.'),
  t(10, '## My Province, My Story\n\nYour province is not just a place on a map — it is **your home**. It has stories of brave people, important events, and beautiful places.\n\n### Things You Can Do\n\n- **Draw a map** of your province and mark the important places\n- **Make a timeline** of important events in your province\'s history\n- **Interview a family member** about what life was like when they were young\n- **Write a paragraph** about why you are proud of your province\n\nRemember:\n- History is not just about famous people and big events\n- **Ordinary people** also make history every day\n- **Your family\'s story** is part of your province\'s history\n- We can learn from the past to make our province a better place for everyone\n\nBy knowing the history of our province, we understand who we are and where we come from. This knowledge helps us to be **responsible citizens** who care about our community.'),
];

