// --- Lesson 1: The San People ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Who Were the San?\n\nThe **San** people are believed to be the **first people** of southern Africa. They have lived in this part of the world for **thousands and thousands of years** — longer than any other group.\n\nThe San were **hunter-gatherers**. This means they got their food by:\n- **Hunting** wild animals like springbok, eland, and rabbits\n- **Gathering** wild plants, berries, roots, and insects\n\nThey did **not** farm or keep animals. Instead, they moved from place to place to find food and water. This way of living is called being **nomadic**.\n\n### Where Did the San Live?\n\nThe San once lived across most of southern Africa. Today, small groups of San people still live in:\n- The **Kalahari Desert** in Botswana and Namibia\n- Parts of South Africa and Namibia\n\nThe San are also called **Bushmen**, but many San people prefer to be called by their own names.'),
  q(2, 'What does "hunter-gatherer" mean?',
    ['People who hunt wild animals and gather wild plants for food', 'People who buy food from shops', 'People who farm and keep cattle', 'People who fish in the sea'], 0,
    'Hunter-gatherers get all their food from nature. They hunt wild animals and collect (gather) wild plants, fruits, berries, nuts, roots, and insects. They do not grow crops or keep farm animals.',
    ['Think about the two words: "hunter" and "gatherer". What do each of them mean?']),
  t(3, '## How the San Found Food\n\n### Hunting\n\nThe men usually did the hunting. They were **very skilled** trackers who could follow animal footprints for many kilometres.\n\n- They used **bows and arrows** to hunt\n- The arrow tips were dipped in **poison** made from plants or beetle larvae (baby beetles)\n- They also used **snares** (traps) to catch small animals\n- Sometimes they hunted in **groups** to catch bigger animals\n\n### Gathering\n\nThe women and children gathered plant foods. Gathering gave the San **most of their food** — about 80%!\n\n| Food Gathered | How |\n|---------------|-----|\n| **Roots and bulbs** | Dug up with a digging stick |\n| **Berries and fruits** | Picked from bushes and trees |\n| **Nuts** | Collected from the ground |\n| **Insects** | Locusts, caterpillars, and wild honey |\n| **Wild melons** | Found in the Kalahari — these provided water too! |\n\nThe **digging stick** was one of the San\'s most important tools. It was a sharpened stick used to dig up roots and bulbs from the ground.'),
  fb(4, 'San arrow tips were dipped in ___ to make them more effective. Women dug up roots using a ___ stick.',
    ['poison', 'digging'],
    'The San used poison from plants or beetle larvae on their arrow tips. Even if the first arrow did not kill the animal, the poison would slowly weaken it so the hunters could catch it. The digging stick was a simple but very useful tool — a sharpened wooden stick for digging up underground roots and bulbs.'),
  t(5, '## How the San Lived Together\n\nThe San lived in **small groups** called **bands**. Each band had about **25 to 50 people** who were all related to each other — like a big family.\n\n### Important Things About San Bands\n\n| Feature | What It Means |\n|---------|---------------|\n| **No chief** | Decisions were made by talking together — everyone had a say |\n| **Sharing** | All food was shared equally — no one went hungry |\n| **Moving** | They moved to new places to find food, water, and shelter |\n| **Shelters** | Simple huts made from sticks and grass — easy to build and leave |\n| **Equality** | Men and women were respected equally |\n\n### San Shelters\n\nBecause the San moved often, they built simple shelters that were easy to make:\n- Made from **bent branches** covered with **grass or leaves**\n- In caves and rock overhangs, they did not need to build — the rock was their roof!\n- In the Kalahari, they sometimes built **wind screens** from branches to keep out the wind'),
  q(6, 'How did the San make decisions in their bands?',
    ['Everyone talked together and shared their ideas — there was no chief', 'A king made all the decisions', 'Only the oldest woman decided everything', 'They voted by raising their hands'], 0,
    'The San had a very fair way of making decisions. There was no chief or king. Instead, everyone in the band could share their opinions. They talked and discussed until they agreed. Elders were respected for their wisdom, but they could not force others to do things.',
    ['Think about how your family makes decisions together.']),
  t(7, '## San Tools and Clothes\n\n### Tools\n\nThe San made their tools from things they found in nature:\n\n| Tool | Made From | Used For |\n|------|-----------|----------|\n| **Bow and arrows** | Wood and animal sinew (string) | Hunting animals |\n| **Digging stick** | Hard wood, sharpened | Digging up roots and bulbs |\n| **Stone scrapers** | Flaked stone | Cleaning animal skins |\n| **Ostrich egg water bottles** | Ostrich eggs with a hole | Carrying and storing water |\n| **Bags** | Animal skin | Carrying food and belongings |\n\n### Clothes\n\nThe San wore very simple clothes because of the hot climate:\n- Clothes were made from **animal skins** (leather)\n- Women wore a leather **apron** and sometimes a **cloak** (kaross)\n- Men wore a leather **loincloth**\n- In cold weather, they wrapped themselves in animal skin **blankets**\n- They decorated themselves with **beads** made from ostrich eggshell pieces'),
  fb(8, 'The San stored and carried water in ___ egg shells. They made beads from ___ eggshell pieces.',
    ['ostrich', 'ostrich'],
    'Ostrich eggs were very useful to the San. They carefully made a small hole in the egg, emptied it, and then used the shell as a water container. They also broke pieces of ostrich eggshell into small circles and made holes in them to string together as beautiful beads for necklaces.'),
  q(9, 'What did the San use to carry water in the Kalahari?',
    ['Ostrich egg shells', 'Plastic bottles', 'Clay pots', 'Metal cans'], 0,
    'The Kalahari is a very dry place, so water was precious. The San used ostrich egg shells as water containers. They would fill the eggs with water, seal the hole with grass, and bury them in the sand to have water when they returned to that area later.',
    ['Think about what natural things could hold water in the wild.']),
  t(10, '## The San Today\n\nThe San people have lived in southern Africa for longer than anyone else. They are truly the **first people** of this land.\n\nSadly, over the years, the San lost most of their land:\n- First to the **Khoikhoi** herders who moved in about 2,000 years ago\n- Then to **Bantu-speaking farmers** who came from further north\n- Then to **European colonists** who took land for farming\n- Many San were killed or forced to work on farms\n\n### Remembering the San\n\nEven though there are few San people left living in the traditional way, their legacy is everywhere:\n- South Africa\'s **national motto** is in a San language: **!ke e: /xarra //ke** — "Diverse People Unite"\n- The **San click languages** influenced other South African languages like Xhosa and Zulu\n- Their **rock art** can be found in mountains and caves across South Africa\n\nWe must respect and remember the San as the first people of our land.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: The Khoikhoi People and Rock Art ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Khoikhoi — "The Real People"\n\nThe **Khoikhoi** (which means "people of people" or "the real people") were **herders** who came to southern Africa about **2,000 years ago**.\n\nThe Khoikhoi were different from the San because they:\n- Kept **cattle and sheep** as farm animals\n- Lived in **bigger groups** that stayed in one area for longer\n- Had **chiefs** who led their communities\n\nHere is how the San and Khoikhoi were different:\n\n| | San | Khoikhoi |\n|--|-----|----------|\n| **Food** | Hunted wild animals, gathered plants | Drank milk, ate meat from their cattle and sheep |\n| **Movement** | Always moving (nomadic) | Moved sometimes to find grazing for cattle |\n| **Leaders** | No chief — everyone decided together | Had chiefs and elders |\n| **Wealth** | Shared everything equally | Cattle = wealth — more cattle meant you were richer |\n| **Homes** | Simple stick shelters | Dome-shaped huts called matjieshuise |'),
  q(2, 'What is the meaning of the name "Khoikhoi"?',
    ['"People of people" or "the real people"', '"People of the river"', '"People who hunt animals"', '"People of the mountain"'], 0,
    'The Khoikhoi called themselves "people of people" which showed they were proud of who they were. Unlike the San who were hunter-gatherers, the Khoikhoi were herders who kept cattle and sheep.'),
  t(3, '## How the Khoikhoi Lived\n\n### Their Homes — Matjieshuise\n\nThe Khoikhoi lived in dome-shaped huts called **matjieshuise** (mat houses):\n- A frame of **bent wooden poles**\n- Covered with **woven reed mats**\n- Could be **taken apart** and moved to a new place\n- Arranged in a **circle** around a central area for the cattle (called a **kraal**)\n\n### Cattle — The Most Important Thing\n\nCattle were at the centre of Khoikhoi life:\n- **Milk** was their main daily food\n- **Meat** was eaten at special celebrations\n- **Wealth** was measured by how many cattle you had\n- **Lobola** (bride price) — cattle were given to the bride\'s family at a wedding\n- Cattle were used to **carry things** when the group moved\n\n### Other Food\n\nThe Khoikhoi also:\n- Gathered wild plants, roots, and berries (like the San)\n- Hunted wild animals sometimes\n- Fished along the coast\n- Made food from **sour milk** (like amasi)'),
  fb(4, 'Khoikhoi huts were called ___. Their cattle were kept at night in a circle called a ___.',
    ['matjieshuise', 'kraal'],
    'Matjieshuise (mat houses) were clever homes — they could be packed up and moved when the group needed fresh grazing land for their cattle. The kraal was the fenced area in the middle of the settlement where cattle slept safely at night, protected from wild animals and thieves.'),
  t(5, '## San Rock Art\n\nOne of the most amazing things the San left behind is their **rock art** — paintings on rock surfaces and in caves.\n\nThere are **thousands** of rock art sites across South Africa, especially in the mountains like the **Drakensberg**.\n\n### What Did the San Paint?\n\n| Subject | Examples |\n|---------|----------|\n| **Animals** | Eland (the most common), springbok, elephants, rhinos |\n| **Hunting scenes** | People with bows chasing animals |\n| **Dancing** | The trance dance (healing dance) |\n| **People** | Men, women, and children doing daily activities |\n| **Handprints** | Made by blowing paint over a hand on the rock |\n\n### How Did They Make Paint?\n\nThe San made their paint from natural materials:\n- **Red and brown** — from a type of rock called ochre\n- **White** — from bird droppings or clay\n- **Black** — from charcoal (burnt wood)\n- These were mixed with **animal fat** to make the paint stick\n\nSome San rock paintings are **thousands of years old** and can still be seen today!'),
  q(6, 'Which animal appears most often in San rock art?',
    ['The eland', 'The lion', 'The elephant', 'The giraffe'], 0,
    'The eland was the most important animal for the San. They believed it had special spiritual power. The eland appears in their rock paintings more than any other animal. It was connected to many San ceremonies and beliefs.',
    ['Think about which animal was most special to the San.']),
  t(7, '## The Trance Dance\n\nThe **trance dance** (also called the healing dance) was the most important ceremony for the San.\n\n### How It Worked\n\n1. The whole band gathered together\n2. **Women** sat in a circle, clapping and singing special songs\n3. **Healers** (medicine people) danced around the circle for a long time\n4. The dancing and singing helped the healers go into a **trance** — a dream-like state\n5. In the trance, healers believed they could **heal sick people** and speak to spirits\n\nMany rock paintings show the trance dance. You can see people dancing, bending over, and sometimes with lines coming from their noses (nosebleeds were common during the trance).\n\n### Why Rock Art Matters\n\nSan rock art is **precious heritage** that belongs to all South Africans:\n- It teaches us about how the San lived\n- It is **beautiful art** created thousands of years ago\n- The rock art sites in the **Drakensberg** are a **UNESCO World Heritage Site**\n- We must **protect** these paintings — never touch or damage them!'),
  fb(8, 'During the trance dance, ___ sat in a circle clapping and singing. The Drakensberg rock art sites are a UNESCO World ___ Site.',
    ['women', 'Heritage'],
    'Women played a very important role in the trance dance. Their rhythmic clapping and singing helped the healers enter a trance state. The San rock art in the uKhahlamba-Drakensberg mountains is so important that it has been declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site — a place of outstanding value for all of humanity.'),
  q(9, 'Why must we protect San rock art?',
    ['It is precious heritage that teaches us about the first people of South Africa and cannot be replaced', 'Because it is just old paint on rocks', 'Because the government will give us money', 'Because it looks nice in photographs'], 0,
    'San rock art is irreplaceable. These paintings are thousands of years old and tell us about the lives, beliefs, and culture of the very first people who lived in southern Africa. Once damaged, they can never be fixed. They are part of our heritage as South Africans.'),
  t(10, '## The Khoisan Legacy\n\nTogether, the San and Khoikhoi are called the **Khoisan** people. They share similar languages that use **click sounds**.\n\n### What They Left Behind\n\n- **Click languages** — the Khoisan languages use click sounds. These clicks were adopted by Xhosa, Zulu, and other South African languages\n- **Place names** — many South African place names come from Khoisan languages: Karoo ("land of thirst"), Knysna, Tsitsikamma\n- **Rock art** — thousands of rock art sites across southern Africa\n- **National motto** — South Africa\'s motto !ke e: /xarra //ke is in the /Xam San language\n- **Coat of arms** — the two human figures on South Africa\'s coat of arms are in San rock art style\n\n### Respecting the First People\n\nThe San and Khoikhoi lost much of their land and way of life over the centuries. Today, South Africa works to:\n- **Recognise** the Khoisan as the first peoples\n- **Protect** their heritage sites and rock art\n- **Preserve** their languages and cultural knowledge\n\nRemembering the Khoisan helps us understand that South Africa\'s history goes back thousands of years, long before any other group arrived.'),
];

