// --- Lesson 1: Learning About the Past ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## How Do We Learn About the Past?\n\nHistory is the story of what happened in the past. But how do we find out about things that happened a long time ago — sometimes before anyone alive today was born?\n\nWe use **sources of information** — clues and evidence that tell us about the past.\n\n### Types of Sources\n\nThere are different kinds of sources:\n\n| Type of Source | What It Is | Examples |\n|----------------|------------|----------|\n| **Written sources** | Things that are written down | Books, letters, diaries, newspapers, school reports |\n| **Oral sources** | Stories told by mouth | Grandparents\' stories, songs, praise poems, legends |\n| **Visual sources** | Things we can see | Old photographs, paintings, maps, rock art |\n| **Physical sources** | Objects we can touch | Old tools, pots, coins, buildings, clothing |\n\nAll of these sources help us **piece together** the story of the past — like solving a puzzle!'),
  q(2, 'What is an "oral source" of history?',
    ['Stories and information told by mouth, such as grandparents\' stories and songs', 'Old photographs found in a museum', 'Books about famous people', 'Ancient tools dug up from the ground'], 0,
    'Oral sources are information passed down by speaking and listening. Before people could write, they remembered important events by telling stories, singing songs, and reciting praise poems. Grandparents telling you stories about their childhood is an example of an oral source.',
    ['Think about what the word "oral" means — it comes from the Latin word for "mouth".']),
  t(3, '## Oral History — Stories Passed Down\n\n**Oral history** is when people share stories about the past by **telling them**, not writing them down.\n\nIn South Africa, oral history has always been very important. Many African communities did not write things down — instead, they had **storytellers** who remembered the history of their people and passed it on to the next generation.\n\n### Examples of Oral History in South Africa\n\n- **Praise poems** (izibongo) — poems about chiefs, leaders, or important events\n- **Legends and stories** — tales about how things began or why things happen\n- **Songs** — many songs tell stories about the past, like struggle songs from the apartheid era\n- **Family stories** — your granny telling you about what school was like when she was young\n\n### Why Oral History Matters\n\n- It tells us about people whose stories were **never written down**\n- It shares **feelings and experiences** that books might miss\n- It keeps **languages and cultures** alive\n- It connects us to our **ancestors** (people who lived before us)\n\n**Important:** Oral history can change slightly each time it is told, so historians compare oral stories with other sources to check the facts.'),
  fb(4, 'Praise poems in the Zulu tradition are called ___. Oral history is passed down by ___ and listening, not writing.',
    ['izibongo', 'telling'],
    'Izibongo are praise poems that celebrate important people and events. They are a powerful form of oral history in Zulu and other South African cultures. Oral history depends on people telling stories to each other — from generation to generation — keeping the past alive through words.'),
  t(5, '## Old Photographs and Pictures\n\n**Photographs** are very useful sources of history. They show us exactly what things looked like in the past.\n\n### What Can Old Photos Tell Us?\n\n| What We See | What It Tells Us |\n|-------------|------------------|\n| **Clothes people wore** | How fashion and daily life have changed |\n| **Buildings** | What towns and cities used to look like |\n| **Transport** | Old cars, ox wagons, horse carts — how people travelled |\n| **Events** | Weddings, school days, sports events, protests |\n| **People** | What our ancestors looked like, how they spent their time |\n\n### Looking at Photos Carefully\n\nWhen you look at an old photograph, ask yourself:\n- **When** was this photo taken? (Look at the clothes, cars, or buildings for clues)\n- **Where** was it taken?\n- **Who** is in the photo?\n- **What** is happening?\n- **Why** might someone have taken this photo?\n\nThese are the same questions historians ask when they study any source!'),
  q(6, 'Why are old photographs useful for learning about history?',
    ['They show us exactly what people, places, and events looked like in the past', 'They are always in colour so they are easy to understand', 'They were taken by historians', 'They always have written explanations on the back'], 0,
    'Old photographs are powerful sources because they freeze a moment in time. We can see the clothes people wore, the buildings they lived in, and the events they experienced. Even though old photos are often in black and white, they give us a window into the past.',
    ['Think about looking at photos of your parents when they were your age.']),
  t(7, '## Museums and Artefacts\n\n### What Is a Museum?\n\nA **museum** is a place where important objects from the past are kept and displayed so people can learn from them. Museums protect these objects and help us understand history.\n\n### Types of Museums in South Africa\n\n| Museum | Where | What It Shows |\n|--------|-------|---------------|\n| **Apartheid Museum** | Johannesburg | The history of apartheid |\n| **Robben Island Museum** | Cape Town | Where Nelson Mandela was imprisoned |\n| **Ditsong Museum** | Pretoria | Natural history, dinosaurs, and fossils |\n| **District Six Museum** | Cape Town | The forced removals during apartheid |\n| **Local museums** | Many towns | The history of your local area |\n\n### What Are Artefacts?\n\n**Artefacts** are objects made or used by people in the past. They can be:\n- **Stone tools** used by the San thousands of years ago\n- **Clay pots** used for cooking and storing food\n- **Coins** and **money** from long ago\n- **Clothing** and **jewellery**\n- **Weapons** like spears and shields\n\nArtefacts are like **clues** that help us understand how people lived, what they ate, and what was important to them.'),
  fb(8, 'Objects made or used by people in the past are called ___. ___ Island near Cape Town is a museum where Nelson Mandela was imprisoned.',
    ['artefacts', 'Robben'],
    'Artefacts are any objects from the past that tell us about how people lived. They can be as simple as a clay pot or as grand as a gold necklace. Robben Island, near Cape Town, is where Nelson Mandela spent 18 of his 27 years in prison. Today it is a museum and UNESCO World Heritage Site.'),
  q(9, 'What are artefacts?',
    ['Objects made or used by people in the past that help us learn about history', 'Only very old things found in the ground', 'Paintings in an art gallery', 'Things you can buy in a museum shop'], 0,
    'Artefacts are any objects from the past — whether they are ancient stone tools or a school report from 50 years ago. They tell us about how people lived, what they used, and what was important to them. Artefacts can be found in museums, in the ground, or even in your grandparents\' cupboards!'),
  t(10, '## Becoming a History Detective\n\nWhen historians study the past, they work like **detectives**. They look at clues (sources) and try to figure out what happened.\n\nYou can be a history detective too! Here is how:\n\n### Steps to Research Local History\n\n1. **Choose a topic** — for example, the history of your school, your street, or your family\n2. **Ask questions** — When was my school built? Who were the first students? What did the area look like before?\n3. **Find sources** — Talk to older people, look for old photos, visit the library or museum\n4. **Record what you find** — Write down the stories, make copies of photos, draw pictures\n5. **Share your findings** — Tell your class, make a poster, or write a short report\n\n### Tips for Interviewing People\n\nWhen you talk to an older person about the past:\n- Be **polite** and **respectful**\n- Ask **open questions** (questions that need more than just "yes" or "no")\n- **Listen carefully** and do not interrupt\n- **Write down** or record what they say (ask permission first!)\n- Say **thank you** when you are finished\n\nEvery person has a story. Your family\'s story is part of South Africa\'s history!'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Timelines and Putting History in Order ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## What Is a Timeline?\n\nA **timeline** is a drawing that shows events in the **order they happened**, from oldest to newest. It helps us see the "big picture" of history.\n\nImagine a long line:\n- On the **left side** (or top) are the **oldest** events — things that happened a long time ago\n- On the **right side** (or bottom) are the **newest** events — things that happened recently\n\n### Example: A Simple Timeline of South Africa\n\n| Date | Event |\n|------|-------|\n| **Thousands of years ago** | San hunter-gatherers live across southern Africa |\n| **About 2,000 years ago** | Khoikhoi herders arrive |\n| **1652** | Dutch settlers arrive at the Cape |\n| **1867** | Diamonds discovered near Kimberley |\n| **1886** | Gold discovered on the Witwatersrand |\n| **1948** | Apartheid begins |\n| **1976** | Soweto Uprising |\n| **1990** | Nelson Mandela released from prison |\n| **1994** | First democratic election — Mandela becomes president |\n| **2010** | South Africa hosts the FIFA World Cup |'),
  q(2, 'In a timeline, where do we put the oldest events?',
    ['On the left side (or at the top) of the timeline', 'On the right side (or at the bottom) of the timeline', 'In the middle of the timeline', 'Anywhere — it does not matter'], 0,
    'A timeline shows events in chronological order (the order they happened). The oldest events go on the left (or top), and the newest events go on the right (or bottom). This helps us see what happened first, second, third, and so on.',
    ['Think about reading a number line in Maths — smallest numbers are on the left.']),
  t(3, '## Making Your Own Timeline\n\nYou can make a timeline about anything — your life, your school, your family, or an important topic in history.\n\n### How to Make a Timeline\n\n1. **Draw a long straight line** across your page (or down the page)\n2. **Mark the dates** along the line, evenly spaced\n3. **Write the events** next to the correct dates\n4. **Add pictures** if you can — this makes your timeline more interesting\n5. **Give your timeline a title**\n\n### Example: My Life Timeline\n\n| Year | Event |\n|------|-------|\n| 2016 | I was born |\n| 2017 | I said my first word |\n| 2019 | I started crèche |\n| 2022 | I started Grade 1 |\n| 2024 | I started Grade 3 |\n| 2025 | I am now in Grade 4 |\n\n### Important Words\n\n- **Chronological order** — putting things in the order they happened, from first to last\n- **Decade** — a period of 10 years\n- **Century** — a period of 100 years (the 21st century started in 2001)\n- **CE** — Common Era (used instead of AD)\n- **BCE** — Before Common Era (used instead of BC)'),
  fb(4, 'Putting events in the order they happened is called ___ order. A period of 100 years is called a ___.',
    ['chronological', 'century'],
    'Chronological order means arranging events from the earliest to the most recent — the order in which they actually happened. A century is 100 years (for example, the 1900s is the 20th century). We are now living in the 21st century.'),
  t(5, '## Comparing Past and Present\n\nOne of the most interesting things about history is seeing how things have **changed** over time.\n\n### How School Has Changed\n\n| | In the Past (your grandparents\' time) | Today |\n|--|---------------------------------------|-------|\n| **Writing** | Chalk and slate boards | Pens, pencils, and sometimes tablets or computers |\n| **Getting to school** | Walking long distances, donkey carts | Walking, cars, buses, taxis |\n| **Uniform** | Often barefoot, simple clothes | Full school uniform with shoes |\n| **Subjects** | Basic reading, writing, maths | Many subjects including technology |\n| **Language** | Often taught in only English or Afrikaans | Now taught in many languages |\n\n### How Transport Has Changed\n\n| Past | Today |\n|------|-------|\n| Walking | Cars, buses, taxis |\n| Horse and cart | Trucks and delivery vans |\n| Ox wagon | The Gautrain and fast trains |\n| Sailing ships | Aeroplanes and container ships |\n\nSome things change, but some things stay the same. People still need food, water, shelter, and love — just like they did hundreds of years ago!'),
  q(6, 'What is a decade?',
    ['A period of 10 years', 'A period of 100 years', 'A period of 1,000 years', 'A period of 50 years'], 0,
    'A decade is 10 years. For example, the 2020s is the decade from 2020 to 2029. Two decades equal 20 years, and 10 decades make up one century (100 years).',
    ['The word "deca" means ten — like a decagon has ten sides.']),
  t(7, '## Heritage Sites — Protecting the Past\n\nA **heritage site** is an important place that is protected because it helps us remember the past. South Africa has many heritage sites.\n\n### Examples of Heritage Sites\n\n| Heritage Site | Province | Why It Is Important |\n|---------------|----------|--------------------|\n| **Robben Island** | Western Cape | Where political prisoners, including Nelson Mandela, were held |\n| **Cradle of Humankind** | Gauteng | Where fossils of early humans were found |\n| **Mapungubwe** | Limpopo | An ancient African kingdom that traded with far-away countries |\n| **iSimangaliso Wetland Park** | KwaZulu-Natal | A beautiful natural area with lakes, beaches, and wildlife |\n| **Drakensberg Rock Art** | KwaZulu-Natal | San rock paintings that are thousands of years old |\n\nSome of these places are **UNESCO World Heritage Sites** — this means the whole world agrees they are important and must be protected.\n\n### How Can We Help Protect Heritage Sites?\n\n- **Do not damage** rock art, old buildings, or artefacts\n- **Do not litter** at heritage sites\n- **Tell others** about why these places are important\n- **Visit** heritage sites and learn from them'),
  fb(8, 'Important places that are protected because they help us remember the past are called ___ sites. UNESCO stands for the United Nations Educational, Scientific and ___ Organization.',
    ['heritage', 'Cultural'],
    'Heritage sites are places that have special historical, cultural, or natural importance. They are protected so that future generations can also learn from them. UNESCO (United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization) identifies World Heritage Sites that are important to all of humanity.'),
  q(9, 'Why is it important to protect heritage sites?',
    ['So that future generations can learn about the past and understand where they come from', 'Because the government makes us protect them', 'Because they are good places for holidays', 'Because they make money from tourists'], 0,
    'Heritage sites connect us to our past. If they are damaged or destroyed, we lose important information about how people lived, what happened, and who we are. Protecting them ensures that your children and grandchildren can also learn from these special places.'),
  t(10, '## You Are Part of History\n\nHistory is not just about famous people and big events that happened long ago. **You** are part of history too!\n\n### Your Personal History\n\nEveryone has a personal history:\n- The day you were born\n- Your first day at school\n- Important family events — weddings, birthdays, celebrations\n- Things that happened in your community\n\n### How to Keep Your Own History\n\n- **Keep a diary or journal** — write down what happens in your life\n- **Take photographs** — they will be valuable to look at when you are older\n- **Collect things** — ticket stubs, drawings, report cards — these are your artefacts!\n- **Listen to family stories** — ask your parents and grandparents about their lives\n- **Draw your family tree** — a picture that shows your family members going back to your grandparents and great-grandparents\n\n### Remember\n\n- The past helps us understand the present\n- We can learn from the **mistakes** of the past\n- We can be inspired by the **achievements** of the past\n- Every person\'s story matters — including yours!\n\nYou are making history every day. What story will you tell?'),
];

