// --- Lesson 1: What Is Heritage? ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Is Heritage?\n\n**Heritage** is everything we receive from the past, enjoy in the present, and pass on to the future. It includes the things our ancestors created and the traditions they practised.\n\nHeritage can be many things:\n\n| Type of Heritage | Examples |\n|------------------|----------|\n| **Natural heritage** | Mountains, rivers, forests, wildlife, the ocean |\n| **Cultural heritage** | Languages, songs, dances, food, clothing, ceremonies |\n| **Built heritage** | Old buildings, monuments, bridges, churches |\n| **Intangible heritage** | Stories, beliefs, skills, knowledge passed down through generations |\n\n### Why Is Heritage Important?\n\n- It tells us **who we are** and where we come from\n- It connects us to our **ancestors** (the people who came before us)\n- It helps us understand and respect **other cultures**\n- It makes South Africa a **unique** and special place\n- It is something we must **protect** for future generations'),
  q(2, 'Which of these is an example of cultural heritage?',
    ['Languages, songs, dances, and traditional food', 'Mountains and rivers', 'Old buildings and bridges', 'Cars and computers'], 0,
    'Cultural heritage includes the traditions, customs, and practices that are passed down through generations. Languages, songs, dances, traditional food, and ceremonies are all examples of cultural heritage. These are things that make each culture unique and special.',
    ['Think about the special things your family does that were passed down from your grandparents.']),
  t(3, '## South Africa\'s National Symbols\n\nSouth Africa has special **national symbols** that represent our country and our heritage. Every South African should know them!\n\n### The South African Flag\n\nOur flag was first used on **27 April 1994** — the day of South Africa\'s first democratic election.\n\nThe flag has **six colours**:\n- **Black** — represents the people of South Africa\n- **Green** — represents the land and farming\n- **Gold/Yellow** — represents the natural resources (like gold)\n- **Red** — represents the blood shed during the struggle for freedom\n- **White** — represents peace\n- **Blue** — represents the sky and the ocean\n\nThe **"Y" shape** on the flag shows different groups coming together and moving forward as one nation.\n\n### The National Anthem\n\nSouth Africa\'s national anthem is unique because it is sung in **five languages**: Xhosa, Zulu, Sesotho, Afrikaans, and English. It combines two old songs:\n- **Nkosi Sikelel\' iAfrika** ("God Bless Africa") — written in 1897\n- **Die Stem van Suid-Afrika** ("The Call of South Africa") — the old national anthem'),
  fb(4, 'South Africa\'s flag has ___ colours. The national anthem is sung in ___ languages.',
    ['six', 'five'],
    'The South African flag has six colours: black, green, gold, red, white, and blue. Each colour has a special meaning. The national anthem is unique in the world because it is sung in five of South Africa\'s 11 official languages — Xhosa, Zulu, Sesotho, Afrikaans, and English.'),
  t(5, '## The Coat of Arms and Motto\n\n### South Africa\'s Coat of Arms\n\nThe **coat of arms** is the official badge of South Africa. It tells the story of our country through its symbols:\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Secretary bird** | Protection and strength — its wings are spread wide |\n| **Rising sun** | A new beginning, brightness, and knowledge |\n| **Protea** | South Africa\'s national flower — beauty and growth |\n| **Ears of wheat** | Farming, food, and growth |\n| **Elephant tusks** | Wisdom and strength |\n| **Shield and spear** | Defence and protection (not war) |\n| **Two San rock art figures** | Our first people, unity, and togetherness |\n| **The motto** | !ke e: /xarra //ke |\n\n### The National Motto\n\n**!ke e: /xarra //ke** means **"Diverse People Unite"** in the /Xam San language.\n\nThis motto was chosen to honour the **San people** — the first people of South Africa — and to remind us that although we are different, we are one nation.\n\nThe "!" and "//" are **click sounds** from the San language.'),
  q(6, 'What does South Africa\'s motto "!ke e: /xarra //ke" mean?',
    ['"Diverse People Unite" — different people coming together as one', '"South Africa is the best country"', '"We are the rainbow people"', '"Freedom for everyone"'], 0,
    'The motto means "Diverse People Unite." It is written in the /Xam San language to honour the first people of South Africa. It reminds us that South Africa has many different cultures, languages, and peoples, but we are all one nation working together.'),
  t(7, '## Other National Symbols\n\nSouth Africa has many other national symbols:\n\n| Symbol | What It Is |\n|--------|------------|\n| **National flower** | King Protea — a large, beautiful flower |\n| **National bird** | Blue Crane — a tall, graceful bird |\n| **National fish** | Galjoen — found along the South African coast |\n| **National animal** | Springbok — also the name of our rugby team |\n| **National tree** | Real Yellowwood — a tall, ancient tree |\n| **National day** | Freedom Day — 27 April (celebrates the first democratic election in 1994) |\n\n### Why Do Countries Have National Symbols?\n\nNational symbols help people:\n- Feel **proud** of their country\n- Feel **united** as one nation\n- **Remember** important parts of their heritage\n- Show the world what their country stands for'),
  fb(8, 'South Africa\'s national flower is the ___. The national animal is the ___.',
    ['King Protea', 'Springbok'],
    'The King Protea is a large, striking flower that grows naturally in the Western Cape. The Springbok is a graceful antelope that can jump very high. The Springbok is also the nickname of the South African national rugby team, known as the Springboks.'),
  q(9, 'When is Freedom Day celebrated in South Africa?',
    ['27 April — it celebrates the first democratic election in 1994', '16 June — Youth Day', '24 September — Heritage Day', '10 December — Human Rights Day'], 0,
    'Freedom Day is celebrated on 27 April every year. This was the date of South Africa\'s first democratic election in 1994, when all South Africans — regardless of race — could vote for the first time. It marked the end of apartheid and the birth of our democracy.'),
  t(10, '## Respecting Our Heritage\n\nSouth Africa is often called the **"Rainbow Nation"** because of our many different cultures, languages, and peoples. This diversity is our greatest heritage.\n\n### South Africa\'s 11 Official Languages\n\n| Language | Mainly Spoken In |\n|----------|------------------|\n| **Zulu** (isiZulu) | KwaZulu-Natal, Gauteng |\n| **Xhosa** (isiXhosa) | Eastern Cape, Western Cape |\n| **Afrikaans** | Western Cape, Northern Cape, Free State |\n| **English** | Across the country |\n| **Sepedi** (Northern Sotho) | Limpopo, Gauteng |\n| **Setswana** | North West, Gauteng |\n| **Sesotho** (Southern Sotho) | Free State, Gauteng |\n| **Xitsonga** | Limpopo, Mpumalanga |\n| **siSwati** | Mpumalanga |\n| **Tshivenda** | Limpopo |\n| **isiNdebele** | Mpumalanga, Gauteng |\n\nHaving 11 official languages shows that South Africa values and respects all its peoples. Every language carries its own heritage — stories, songs, and wisdom passed down through generations.\n\nWe must respect each other\'s heritage, even when it is different from our own. That is what makes South Africa special!'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Heritage Day and Protecting Heritage ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Heritage Day — 24 September\n\n**Heritage Day** is a public holiday celebrated on **24 September** every year in South Africa. It is a day for all South Africans to celebrate their culture and heritage.\n\n### What Happens on Heritage Day?\n\nPeople celebrate in many different ways:\n- Wearing **traditional clothing** — Zulu, Xhosa, Sotho, Afrikaans, Indian, Malay, and many other styles\n- Cooking and sharing **traditional food**\n- Performing traditional **dances and music**\n- Telling stories about their family and culture\n- Having a **braai** (barbecue) — Heritage Day is also called **National Braai Day**!\n\n### Why Is Heritage Day Important?\n\n- It reminds us to be **proud** of where we come from\n- It encourages us to **learn** about other cultures\n- It brings people from different backgrounds **together**\n- It shows that South Africa\'s **diversity** (having many different cultures) is a strength, not a weakness'),
  q(2, 'When is Heritage Day celebrated in South Africa?',
    ['24 September every year', '27 April every year', '16 June every year', '25 December every year'], 0,
    'Heritage Day is on 24 September. It is a day when all South Africans are encouraged to celebrate their culture and heritage. Many people also call it National Braai Day because South Africans from all backgrounds love to have a braai!',
    ['Hint: It is in the same month as the start of spring in South Africa.']),
  t(3, '## Cultural Heritage in South Africa\n\nSouth Africa is rich in cultural heritage from many different groups. Let us look at some examples:\n\n### Zulu Heritage\n\n- The **Zulu dance** (indlamu) — a powerful stamping dance performed at celebrations\n- **Beadwork** — colourful beads with special meanings and messages\n- **Isicathamiya** — a beautiful style of singing made famous by Ladysmith Black Mambazo\n\n### Xhosa Heritage\n\n- **Umqombothi** — traditional beer brewed from maize and sorghum\n- **Initiation** ceremonies that mark the change from boy to man\n- **Beadwork** and face painting with white clay\n\n### Afrikaans Heritage\n\n- **Potjiekos** — a stew cooked slowly in a three-legged iron pot over a fire\n- **Boeremusiek** — traditional Afrikaans music with an accordion\n- The **Cape Dutch** style of buildings (like Groot Constantia wine estate)\n\n### Cape Malay Heritage\n\n- **Bo-Kaap** in Cape Town — colourful houses and mosques\n- **Koesisters** — a sweet, spicy doughnut-like treat\n- The **Cape Malay Choir** competitions at New Year'),
  fb(4, 'Ladysmith Black Mambazo is famous for a style of Zulu singing called ___. The colourful houses in Cape Town\'s ___ are part of Cape Malay heritage.',
    ['isicathamiya', 'Bo-Kaap'],
    'Isicathamiya is a style of a cappella singing (singing without instruments) that comes from Zulu migrant workers. Ladysmith Black Mambazo won many Grammy Awards performing this music. The Bo-Kaap is a neighbourhood in Cape Town with brightly painted houses, a strong Muslim community, and delicious Cape Malay food.'),
  t(5, '## Indian and Other Heritage in South Africa\n\n### Indian Heritage\n\nIndian people came to South Africa from the **1860s**, mainly to work on the sugar cane farms in KwaZulu-Natal. They brought their own rich heritage:\n- **Diwali** — the festival of lights, celebrated with candles, sweets, and fireworks\n- **Curry and roti** — delicious food that has become a favourite for all South Africans, especially Durban\'s famous **bunny chow**\n- **Temples** — beautiful Hindu temples in KwaZulu-Natal and Gauteng\n\n### Other Heritage Groups\n\n| Group | Heritage Examples |\n|-------|------------------|\n| **Sotho** | Basotho blankets, mokorotlo (straw hat), the reed dance |\n| **Tswana** | Traditional courts (kgotla), sorghum porridge, rain-making ceremonies |\n| **Venda** | The Sacred Lake Fundudzi, domba dance, wood carving |\n| **Tsonga** | Xibelani dance (women shake their hips wearing special skirts) |\n| **Ndebele** | Beautiful geometric house painting, beadwork |\n\nEvery cultural group in South Africa has something beautiful and special to share!'),
  q(6, 'Which cultural group is known for painting their houses with beautiful geometric (shape) patterns?',
    ['The Ndebele people', 'The Zulu people', 'The Cape Malay people', 'The Afrikaans people'], 0,
    'The Ndebele people are famous around the world for their stunning house painting. Women paint the walls of their homes with bright, colourful geometric patterns. This tradition has been passed down from mothers to daughters for generations and is a UNESCO recognised cultural practice.',
    ['Think about colourful shapes painted on the outside of houses.']),
  t(7, '## Protecting Our Heritage\n\nHeritage is precious, and once it is lost, it can never come back. That is why we must work to **protect** our heritage.\n\n### Threats to Heritage\n\n| Threat | How It Damages Heritage |\n|--------|------------------------|\n| **Vandalism** | People damaging rock art, buildings, or monuments |\n| **Development** | New buildings replacing old, important ones |\n| **Neglect** | Not taking care of heritage sites — they fall apart |\n| **Pollution** | Dirty air and acid rain damage old buildings and rock art |\n| **Forgetting** | When languages and traditions are not passed on, they die |\n\n### How Heritage Is Protected in South Africa\n\n- **The South African Heritage Resources Agency (SAHRA)** — protects heritage sites\n- **UNESCO World Heritage Sites** — 10 places in South Africa are protected by the whole world\n- **Heritage Day** — reminds people to value their culture\n- **Museums** — keep artefacts and stories safe\n- **Laws** — it is against the law to damage heritage sites'),
  fb(8, 'South Africa has ___ UNESCO World Heritage Sites. The body that protects heritage sites in South Africa is called ___.',
    ['10', 'SAHRA'],
    'South Africa has 10 UNESCO World Heritage Sites, including Robben Island, the Cradle of Humankind, and the iSimangaliso Wetland Park. SAHRA (South African Heritage Resources Agency) is the government body responsible for protecting important heritage sites and objects across the country.'),
  q(9, 'What happens when a language is no longer spoken or passed on to the next generation?',
    ['The language dies and all its stories, songs, and knowledge are lost forever', 'Nothing happens — it does not matter', 'Someone will write it down later', 'A computer will remember it for us'], 0,
    'When a language dies, we lose much more than just words. We lose all the stories, songs, knowledge, and ways of thinking that were carried in that language. Several Khoisan languages have already been lost. That is why it is so important to speak your home language and pass it on to your children.',
    ['Think about what would happen if no one spoke your home language anymore.']),
  t(10, '## You Are a Heritage Keeper\n\nYou do not have to be a grown-up to protect heritage. There are many things **you** can do:\n\n### How Children Can Protect Heritage\n\n- **Speak your home language** — do not be ashamed of it; it is part of who you are\n- **Learn your family\'s stories** — ask grandparents about the old days and write the stories down\n- **Respect other cultures** — learn about your friends\' traditions and celebrations\n- **Do not vandalise** — never damage old buildings, rock art, or heritage sites\n- **Celebrate Heritage Day** — wear traditional clothes, cook traditional food, share with friends\n- **Visit museums** — learn about the past and appreciate what came before you\n\n### A Rainbow of Heritage\n\nSouth Africa is called the Rainbow Nation because, just like a rainbow has many colours that work together to create something beautiful, South Africa has many cultures that make our country special.\n\nEvery culture adds something beautiful:\n- Zulu strength and dance\n- Xhosa wisdom and storytelling\n- Afrikaans boerekos and hospitality\n- Indian spices and festivals\n- Ndebele art and colour\n- And so many more!\n\nBe proud of your heritage. Share it with others. And always respect the heritage of the people around you.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 5 (Geography – Term 3): Maps and Directions
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: What Is a Map? ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Is a Map?\n\nA **map** is a flat drawing of a place, as if you are looking down from above — like a bird flying over the land.\n\nMaps help us:\n- Find **where** places are\n- See **how far** one place is from another\n- Plan a **journey** or route\n- Learn about different **places** in our country and the world\n\n### Maps vs. Globes\n\n| | Map | Globe |\n|--|-----|-------|\n| **Shape** | Flat (can be on paper or a screen) | Round (like a ball) |\n| **Shows** | Can show the whole world or just a small area | Shows the whole world |\n| **Carrying** | Easy to fold and carry | Difficult to carry around |\n| **Detail** | Can show lots of detail for a small area | Shows less detail |\n| **Accuracy** | Shapes can be slightly stretched | More accurate shape of the Earth |\n\nA **globe** is a round model of the Earth. Because the Earth is round (like a ball), a globe shows the true shape of the continents. A map is flat, so it stretches some shapes slightly.'),
  q(2, 'What is the difference between a map and a globe?',
    ['A map is flat and can show a small area in detail, while a globe is round and shows the whole world', 'A map is always bigger than a globe', 'A globe is flat and a map is round', 'There is no difference — they are the same thing'], 0,
    'The main differences are shape and detail. A map is flat (on paper or a screen) and can show a small area like your neighbourhood in great detail. A globe is round like the Earth and shows the true shapes of continents, but it cannot show much detail.',
    ['Think about what happens when you try to flatten an orange peel — it stretches.']),
  t(3, '## Types of Maps\n\nThere are many different types of maps, and each one shows different information:\n\n| Type of Map | What It Shows |\n|-------------|---------------|\n| **Physical map** | The natural features — mountains, rivers, deserts, oceans |\n| **Political map** | Countries, provinces, borders, and capital cities |\n| **Road map** | Roads, highways, towns — used for travelling |\n| **Weather map** | Temperature, rainfall, wind — what the weather will be |\n| **Topographic map** | Detailed features including hills, rivers, buildings, and roads |\n| **Thematic map** | One specific topic — like population, rainfall, or farming areas |\n\n### Which Map Would You Use?\n\n- Planning a road trip to Durban? — Use a **road map**\n- Wondering if it will rain tomorrow? — Use a **weather map**\n- Learning about the provinces of South Africa? — Use a **political map**\n- Studying the Drakensberg mountains? — Use a **physical map**'),
  fb(4, 'A map that shows mountains, rivers, and deserts is called a ___ map. A map that shows countries and borders is called a ___ map.',
    ['physical', 'political'],
    'Physical maps show natural features like mountains (often in brown), rivers (in blue), deserts (in yellow), and plains (in green). Political maps show human-made borders between countries and provinces, along with capital cities and major towns.'),
  t(5, '## Compass Directions\n\nTo use a map, you need to know **compass directions**. A **compass** is a tool that always points **North**.\n\n### The Four Main Directions (Cardinal Directions)\n\n| Direction | Where It Points |\n|-----------|----------------|\n| **North (N)** | Towards the top of the map |\n| **South (S)** | Towards the bottom of the map |\n| **East (E)** | Towards the right of the map |\n| **West (W)** | Towards the left of the map |\n\n### How to Remember: **N**ever **E**at **S**limy **W**orms!\n\nGoing clockwise (like a clock) from the top: **N — E — S — W**\n\n### In-Between Directions\n\nThere are also directions that are in between the main ones:\n\n| Direction | Where It Points |\n|-----------|----------------|\n| **North-East (NE)** | Between North and East |\n| **North-West (NW)** | Between North and West |\n| **South-East (SE)** | Between South and East |\n| **South-West (SW)** | Between South and West |\n\nThese in-between directions are called **intercardinal** directions.'),
  q(6, 'If North is at the top of a map, where is East?',
    ['On the right side of the map', 'On the left side of the map', 'At the bottom of the map', 'In the middle of the map'], 0,
    'On most maps, North is at the top, South is at the bottom, East is on the right, and West is on the left. A good way to remember is: "Never Eat Slimy Worms" — going clockwise from the top: North, East, South, West.',
    ['Never Eat Slimy Worms — N, E, S, W — start at the top and go clockwise.']),
  t(7, '## Map Symbols and the Key\n\n### What Are Map Symbols?\n\nMaps cannot show everything using words — that would make them too crowded. Instead, maps use **symbols** — small pictures, shapes, or colours that stand for real things.\n\n### Common Map Symbols\n\n| Symbol | What It Means |\n|--------|---------------|\n| **Blue lines** | Rivers and streams |\n| **Blue areas** | Lakes, dams, and the ocean |\n| **Green areas** | Forests, parks, or vegetation |\n| **Brown lines** | Contour lines (showing hills and mountains) |\n| **Black lines** | Roads |\n| **Red or yellow lines** | Major roads or highways |\n| **Small black squares** | Buildings |\n| **A cross (+)** | A church |\n| **An "H"** | A hospital |\n| **A small aeroplane** | An airport |\n\n### The Key (Legend)\n\nThe **key** (also called the **legend**) is a small box on the map that explains what each symbol means. **Always check the key before reading a map!**\n\nWithout the key, you would not know what the symbols on the map represent.'),
  fb(8, 'Small pictures on a map that stand for real things are called ___. The box that explains what the symbols mean is called the ___ or legend.',
    ['symbols', 'key'],
    'Map symbols save space and make maps easier to read. For example, a small blue line is quicker and clearer than writing "river" across the map. The key (or legend) is essential — it is like a dictionary for the map, telling you what each symbol represents.'),
  q(9, 'What should you always check before reading a map?',
    ['The key (legend) — to understand what the symbols mean', 'The price of the map', 'The name of the person who drew the map', 'The colour of the paper'], 0,
    'The key (or legend) is the first thing you should look at when reading a map. It explains what all the symbols, colours, and lines on the map represent. Without understanding the key, you might mistake a river for a road or a park for a lake!'),
  t(10, '## Using Compass Directions in South Africa\n\nLet us practise using compass directions with places in South Africa:\n\n### Where Are the Provinces?\n\n| Province | Direction from the Centre of SA |\n|----------|-------------------------------|\n| **Limpopo** | North |\n| **Western Cape** | South-West |\n| **KwaZulu-Natal** | East |\n| **Northern Cape** | West |\n| **Mpumalanga** | North-East |\n\n### Practice Examples\n\n- Johannesburg is in the **north** of South Africa\n- Cape Town is in the **south-west**\n- Durban is in the **east**\n- The Indian Ocean is to the **east** of South Africa\n- The Atlantic Ocean is to the **west** of South Africa\n- South Africa\'s neighbours to the **north** include Botswana and Zimbabwe\n\n### Fun Fact\n\nDid you know that the sun rises in the **East** and sets in the **West** every day? If you face the sunrise in the morning, North is on your left and South is on your right!'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Drawing and Reading a Simple Map ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Reading a Map Step by Step\n\nReading a map is a skill that gets easier with practice. Here are the steps:\n\n### Step 1: Look at the Title\n\nThe **title** tells you what the map is about. For example:\n- "Map of South Africa\'s Provinces"\n- "Road Map of Gauteng"\n- "Physical Map of Africa"\n\n### Step 2: Find the Key (Legend)\n\nCheck the **key** to understand what the symbols and colours mean.\n\n### Step 3: Find North\n\nLook for the **north arrow** (compass rose) on the map. This tells you which direction is North. If there is no arrow, North is usually at the top.\n\n### Step 4: Use the Scale\n\nThe **scale** tells you how distances on the map relate to real distances. For example:\n- 1 cm on the map = 10 km in real life\n\n### Step 5: Read the Map!\n\nNow you can find places, measure distances, and describe where things are using compass directions.'),
  q(2, 'What does the scale on a map tell you?',
    ['How distances on the map relate to real distances — for example, 1 cm on the map equals 10 km in real life', 'How heavy the map is', 'Who drew the map', 'What colours are used on the map'], 0,
    'The scale is very important because a map is much smaller than the real place it shows. The scale tells you how to convert distances on the map to real distances. For example, if the scale says 1 cm = 10 km, then 3 cm on the map would be 30 km in real life.',
    ['Think about a model car — it is smaller than a real car, but the proportions are the same.']),
  t(3, '## Drawing a Simple Map\n\nYou can draw your own map! Here is how to draw a map of your classroom or your route to school:\n\n### How to Draw a Map\n\n1. **Decide what to map** — your classroom, playground, or route to school\n2. **Look at the place from above** — imagine you are a bird looking down\n3. **Draw the outline** — the shape of the room or area\n4. **Add important features** — desks, doors, windows, buildings, roads\n5. **Use symbols** — small shapes or pictures for different things\n6. **Add a key** — explain what your symbols mean\n7. **Add a north arrow** — show which direction is North\n8. **Give your map a title**\n\n### Tips for Drawing a Good Map\n\n- Use a **ruler** for straight lines\n- Keep things **in proportion** — big things should be bigger on your map\n- Use **colour** to make your map clear (blue for water, green for grass)\n- **Label** important places\n- Make sure your map is **neat** and easy to read'),
  fb(4, 'When drawing a map, you should imagine looking at the place from ___. Every map should have a title, a key, and a ___ arrow.',
    ['above', 'north'],
    'Maps show places as if you are looking down from above — this is called a "bird\'s eye view." Every good map needs three things: a title (what the map shows), a key (what the symbols mean), and a north arrow (which direction is north).'),
  t(5, '## Scale — How Big Is the Real Place?\n\nA map is much **smaller** than the real place it shows. The **scale** tells you how much smaller.\n\n### Types of Scale\n\n| Type | Example | What It Means |\n|------|---------|---------------|\n| **Word scale** | "1 centimetre represents 1 kilometre" | Every 1 cm on the map = 1 km in real life |\n| **Number scale** | 1 : 50 000 | 1 cm on the map = 50 000 cm (500 m) in real life |\n| **Line scale** | A small ruler drawn on the map | You measure against the line to find the real distance |\n\n### Practising Scale\n\nIf a map has a scale of **1 cm = 5 km**:\n\n| Distance on Map | Real Distance |\n|----------------|---------------|\n| 1 cm | 5 km |\n| 2 cm | 10 km |\n| 3 cm | 15 km |\n| 5 cm | 25 km |\n| 10 cm | 50 km |\n\nTo work it out, just **multiply** the map distance by 5!\n\n**Important:** A map with a scale of 1 cm = 1 km shows more detail than a map with a scale of 1 cm = 100 km. The first map shows a small area in great detail. The second map shows a large area with less detail.'),
  q(6, 'If a map has a scale of 1 cm = 5 km, and two towns are 4 cm apart on the map, how far apart are they in real life?',
    ['20 km — because 4 × 5 = 20', '4 km — because 4 × 1 = 4', '9 km — because 4 + 5 = 9', '45 km — because 4 × 5 + 25 = 45'], 0,
    'To find the real distance, multiply the map distance by the scale. The map distance is 4 cm and the scale says 1 cm = 5 km, so 4 × 5 = 20 km. The two towns are 20 km apart in real life.',
    ['Multiply the distance on the map by the number of kilometres each centimetre represents.']),
  t(7, '## Grid References\n\nSome maps have **grid lines** — horizontal and vertical lines that divide the map into squares. These help us describe exactly where something is on the map.\n\n### How Grid References Work\n\nEach square on the map has a **grid reference** — like an address for that square.\n\n- The **horizontal lines** have numbers (1, 2, 3...) going from bottom to top\n- The **vertical lines** have letters (A, B, C...) going from left to right\n\nTo find a grid reference:\n1. First, read the **letter** (go across) — this tells you the column\n2. Then, read the **number** (go up) — this tells you the row\n\n### Example\n\nIf a school is in square **B3**, that means:\n- Column **B** (the second column from the left)\n- Row **3** (the third row from the bottom)\n\n### Easy Way to Remember\n\n**"Along the corridor and up the stairs"**\n- First go **along** (the letter)\n- Then go **up** (the number)'),
  fb(8, 'To read a grid reference, first go ___ (the letter), then go up (the number). The phrase to remember is "along the corridor and up the ___."',
    ['along', 'stairs'],
    'Grid references are read by first going along the bottom of the map to find the correct letter (column), then going up to find the correct number (row). The memory trick "along the corridor and up the stairs" helps you remember the correct order: letter first, number second.'),
  q(9, 'What is a grid reference?',
    ['A code using letters and numbers that tells you the exact position of something on a map', 'The title of a map', 'The distance between two places', 'A type of compass'], 0,
    'A grid reference is like an address on a map. It uses a letter and a number to identify a specific square on the map. For example, "C4" means the square in column C, row 4. This makes it easy to describe exactly where something is located.',
    ['Think about how you find a seat in a cinema — row letter and seat number!']),
  t(10, '## Map Skills in Everyday Life\n\nMap skills are not just for geography class — you use them in everyday life!\n\n### When Do We Use Maps?\n\n| Situation | Type of Map |\n|-----------|------------|\n| **Finding your way in a shopping mall** | Floor plan (a map of the building) |\n| **Getting to a friend\'s house** | Google Maps or a street map on your phone |\n| **Planning a holiday** | Road map or travel app |\n| **Watching the weather forecast** | Weather map on TV |\n| **Playing treasure hunt** | A hand-drawn map with clues! |\n| **Learning about countries** | Atlas (a book of maps) |\n\n### GPS and Digital Maps\n\nToday, many people use **GPS** (Global Positioning System) on their phones to find their way. Apps like **Google Maps** and **Waze** are digital maps that can:\n- Show your **exact location**\n- Give **directions** step by step\n- Show **traffic** and suggest the fastest route\n- Find **places** like shops, hospitals, and petrol stations\n\nBut it is still important to understand **paper maps** and **compass directions**. Technology can fail — your phone might run out of battery! Knowing how to read a map is a skill that will always be useful.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 6 (Geography – Term 3): Water in South Africa
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Where Does Our Water Come From? ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Water — Our Most Precious Resource\n\n**Water is essential for life.** Every person, animal, and plant on Earth needs water to survive. Without water, we would not be here.\n\nWe use water every day for:\n- **Drinking** and cooking\n- **Washing** our bodies, clothes, and dishes\n- **Growing food** — crops and gardens need water\n- **Animals** — farm animals and pets need water\n- **Industry** — factories use water to make products\n\nBut here is an important fact: **South Africa is a dry country.** We get less rain than most other countries in the world. This means water is very precious, and we must be careful not to waste it.'),
  q(2, 'Why is water considered South Africa\'s most precious resource?',
    ['Because South Africa is a dry country that gets less rain than most other countries', 'Because water is expensive to buy', 'Because we have too much water and need to use it up', 'Because it rains every day in South Africa'], 0,
    'South Africa receives an average of about 500 mm of rain per year, which is much less than the world average of about 860 mm. This makes South Africa a semi-arid (quite dry) country. Water is therefore extremely precious and we cannot afford to waste it.'),
  t(3, '## The Water Cycle\n\nWater does not just appear — it moves in a never-ending circle called the **water cycle**.\n\n### How the Water Cycle Works\n\n| Step | What Happens |\n|------|-------------|\n| **1. Evaporation** | The sun heats water in rivers, dams, and the ocean. The water turns into invisible gas called water vapour and rises into the air. |\n| **2. Condensation** | The water vapour rises high into the sky where it is cold. It cools down and turns back into tiny water droplets, forming clouds. |\n| **3. Precipitation** | When the clouds become heavy with water droplets, the water falls back to Earth as rain (or sometimes hail or snow in cold areas). |\n| **4. Collection** | The rain collects in rivers, dams, lakes, and underground. Some of it flows into the ocean, and the cycle starts again! |\n\n### Key Words\n\n- **Evaporation** — water changing from liquid to gas (water vapour)\n- **Condensation** — water vapour changing back into liquid (forming clouds)\n- **Precipitation** — water falling from clouds (rain, hail, or snow)\n- **Groundwater** — water that soaks into the ground and is stored underground'),
  fb(4, 'When the sun heats water and it turns into gas, this is called ___. When water vapour cools and forms clouds, this is called ___.',
    ['evaporation', 'condensation'],
    'Evaporation happens when heat from the sun turns liquid water into water vapour (gas) that rises into the air. Condensation is the opposite — water vapour cools high in the sky and turns back into tiny water droplets that form clouds. These two processes are key parts of the water cycle.'),
  t(5, '## Rivers and Dams in South Africa\n\n### Important Rivers\n\nSouth Africa has many rivers, but most of them are not very big compared to rivers in other countries.\n\n| River | Where It Flows | Why It Is Important |\n|-------|----------------|--------------------|\n| **Orange River** | From the Drakensberg mountains to the Atlantic Ocean | South Africa\'s longest river (about 2,200 km) |\n| **Vaal River** | Joins the Orange River | Provides water to Gauteng and the Free State |\n| **Limpopo River** | Along the border with Botswana and Zimbabwe | The second-longest river in SA |\n| **Tugela River** | From the Drakensberg to the Indian Ocean | Has the second-highest waterfall in the world! |\n| **Breede River** | Through the Western Cape | Important for farming in the area |\n\n### What Is a Dam?\n\nA **dam** is a wall built across a river to hold back water, creating a large lake. Dams store water so we can use it during dry times.\n\n### Major Dams\n\n- **Gariep Dam** (Free State) — South Africa\'s biggest dam\n- **Vaal Dam** (Gauteng/Free State) — supplies water to Johannesburg and Pretoria\n- **Sterkfontein Dam** (Free State) — one of the largest by volume\n- **Hartbeespoort Dam** (North West) — popular for recreation near Pretoria'),
  q(6, 'What is South Africa\'s longest river?',
    ['The Orange River — about 2,200 km long', 'The Vaal River — about 1,000 km long', 'The Limpopo River — about 1,750 km long', 'The Tugela River — about 500 km long'], 0,
    'The Orange River is South Africa\'s longest river at about 2,200 km. It starts in the Drakensberg mountains in Lesotho and flows westward all the way to the Atlantic Ocean on the west coast. It was named after the Dutch royal family, the House of Orange.',
    ['Think about the river that shares its name with a colour and a fruit!']),
  t(7, '## Rainfall in South Africa\n\nRain does not fall equally across South Africa. Some areas get a lot of rain, while others get very little.\n\n### Rainfall Patterns\n\n| Area | Rainfall | Reason |\n|------|----------|--------|\n| **Eastern coast** (KwaZulu-Natal) | High — over 1,000 mm per year | Warm, wet air from the Indian Ocean |\n| **Western Cape** | Medium — about 500 mm per year | Winter rainfall from the Atlantic Ocean |\n| **Gauteng** | Medium — about 700 mm per year | Summer thunderstorms |\n| **Northern Cape and Kalahari** | Very low — less than 200 mm per year | Far from the ocean, dry conditions |\n| **Drakensberg mountains** | Very high — over 1,500 mm per year | Mountains force air up, causing rain |\n\n### Summer vs. Winter Rainfall\n\n- Most of South Africa gets rain in **summer** (October to March)\n- The **Western Cape** is different — it gets most of its rain in **winter** (May to August)\n- This is why Cape Town can have water shortages in summer when the dams are low'),
  fb(8, 'Most of South Africa gets rain in ___, but the Western Cape gets most of its rain in ___.',
    ['summer', 'winter'],
    'South Africa has two main rainfall regions. Most of the country (including Gauteng, KwaZulu-Natal, and the Eastern Cape) gets summer rainfall from October to March, often as afternoon thunderstorms. The Western Cape is unusual — it gets winter rainfall from May to August, brought by cold fronts from the Atlantic Ocean.'),
  q(9, 'Which part of South Africa gets the least rainfall?',
    ['The Northern Cape and Kalahari — less than 200 mm per year', 'KwaZulu-Natal — because it is on the coast', 'Gauteng — because it is inland', 'The Drakensberg mountains — because they are so high'], 0,
    'The Northern Cape is South Africa\'s driest province. The Kalahari area in the Northern Cape gets less than 200 mm of rain per year. This is because it is far from the ocean and the air is very dry. By comparison, parts of KwaZulu-Natal get over 1,000 mm per year.',
    ['Think about which part of South Africa is mostly desert.']),
  t(10, '## How Water Gets to Our Taps\n\nHave you ever wondered how water gets from a river or dam to your tap at home? Here is the journey:\n\n### The Water Journey\n\n1. **Collection** — Rain falls into rivers, streams, and dams\n2. **Pumping** — Water is pumped from the dam to a water treatment plant\n3. **Cleaning (treatment)** — The water is cleaned to make it safe to drink:\n   - Dirt and particles are removed\n   - Chemicals are added to kill germs\n   - The water is filtered through sand\n4. **Storage** — Clean water is stored in large tanks and reservoirs\n5. **Distribution** — Water is sent through underground **pipes** to houses, schools, and businesses\n6. **Your tap!** — You turn the tap and clean water comes out\n\n### Water in South Africa\n\nSadly, **not everyone in South Africa has clean water** from a tap. Some people must:\n- Walk long distances to **fetch water** from a river or borehole\n- Use **untreated water** that can make them sick\n- Share a **communal tap** with many other families\n\nThe government is working to provide clean water to everyone, but it is still a big challenge. That is another reason why those of us who have tap water must **never waste it**.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Saving Water ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Why We Must Save Water\n\nSouth Africa is a **water-scarce** country. This means we do not have enough water for everyone, especially when there is a drought (a long time without rain).\n\n### Water Problems in South Africa\n\n| Problem | What It Means |\n|---------|---------------|\n| **Droughts** | Long periods without rain — dams dry up, crops die |\n| **Growing population** | More people need more water, but the amount of water stays the same |\n| **Pollution** | Dirty water from factories and sewage makes rivers and dams unsafe |\n| **Leaking pipes** | Old and broken pipes waste millions of litres of water |\n| **Climate change** | Weather patterns are changing, making droughts more common |\n\n### The Cape Town Water Crisis (2017-2018)\n\nIn 2017-2018, Cape Town almost ran out of water! After three years of very low rainfall, the dams were nearly empty.\n\n- The city warned of **"Day Zero"** — the day when taps would be turned off\n- People were limited to **50 litres per person per day**\n- Car washing and garden watering were banned\n- The city came very close to running out of water\n- Thankfully, people saved water, the rains came, and Day Zero was avoided'),
  q(2, 'What was "Day Zero" in Cape Town?',
    ['The day when water taps would be turned off because the dams were nearly empty', 'The first day of the new year', 'The day Cape Town was founded', 'The day a new dam was built in Cape Town'], 0,
    'During 2017-2018, Cape Town experienced a severe drought. The dams dropped below 20% and the city warned of "Day Zero" — the day when residential water would be switched off. Citizens would have had to queue at collection points for water. Thankfully, water saving and eventual rains prevented it.',
    ['Think about what would happen if there was not enough water in the dams.']),
  t(3, '## How We Use Water\n\nDo you know how much water different activities use? You might be surprised!\n\n### Water Use at Home\n\n| Activity | Water Used |\n|----------|------------|\n| **Flushing a toilet** | About 9 litres per flush |\n| **Showering** (5 minutes) | About 60-80 litres |\n| **Taking a bath** | About 150 litres |\n| **Brushing teeth with tap running** | About 5 litres per minute |\n| **Washing hands** | About 2 litres |\n| **Running a dishwasher** | About 15-25 litres |\n| **Washing machine** | About 50-100 litres per load |\n| **Watering the garden** (hose) | About 15 litres per minute! |\n\n### Think About It\n\nIf you leave the tap running while brushing your teeth for 2 minutes, you waste about **10 litres** of water! That is enough for another person to drink for **5 days**.\n\nA dripping tap can waste **30-50 litres per day** — that adds up to thousands of litres per year!'),
  fb(4, 'Taking a bath uses about ___ litres of water. A dripping tap can waste up to 50 litres per ___.',
    ['150', 'day'],
    'A bath uses about 150 litres of water, which is much more than a short shower (60-80 litres). Even a small drip from a tap wastes water constantly — up to 50 litres per day, which adds up to over 18,000 litres per year! Fixing leaking taps is one of the easiest ways to save water.'),
  t(5, '## Ways to Save Water\n\nEvery person can help save water. Here are things you can do at home and at school:\n\n### Easy Water-Saving Tips\n\n| Tip | How It Saves Water |\n|-----|--------------------|\n| **Turn off the tap** while brushing teeth | Saves about 10 litres each time |\n| **Take short showers** instead of baths | A 2-minute shower uses much less than a bath |\n| **Fix dripping taps** | A drip wastes thousands of litres per year |\n| **Use a cup** of water to rinse when brushing teeth | Instead of running water from the tap |\n| **Water the garden** in the evening, not midday | Less water evaporates when it is cool |\n| **Collect rainwater** in a bucket or tank | Free water for the garden! |\n| **Only flush when needed** | "If it is yellow, let it mellow" |\n| **Full loads only** | Only run the washing machine when it is full |\n| **Reuse water** | Use bath water or rinse water for the garden |\n| **Report leaks** | If you see a leaking pipe in the street, tell the municipality |\n\nRemember: **Every drop counts!**'),
  q(6, 'What is the best time of day to water a garden?',
    ['In the evening when it is cool — less water evaporates', 'At midday when the sun is hottest', 'In the morning when the sun is rising', 'It does not matter what time you water'], 0,
    'The best time to water a garden is in the evening (or early morning) when it is cooler. At midday, the hot sun causes a lot of the water to evaporate (turn into gas) before the plants can absorb it. Watering in the cool of the evening means more water reaches the plant roots.',
    ['Think about what the hot sun does to water.']),
  t(7, '## Water in Different Parts of South Africa\n\nDifferent provinces have different amounts of water and different water challenges:\n\n| Province | Water Situation |\n|----------|----------------|\n| **KwaZulu-Natal** | Good rainfall, many rivers — but flooding can be a problem |\n| **Western Cape** | Winter rainfall only — droughts in summer, like the 2017-2018 crisis |\n| **Gauteng** | Depends on water from the Vaal Dam — millions of people, high demand |\n| **Northern Cape** | Very dry — depends on the Orange River and underground water |\n| **Limpopo** | Some areas have very little water, rural communities often struggle |\n| **Eastern Cape** | Severe droughts have affected cities like Gqeberha (Port Elizabeth) |\n\n### Interesting Water Facts about South Africa\n\n- The **Tugela Falls** in KwaZulu-Natal is the second-highest waterfall in the world (948 metres!)\n- South Africa transfers water from **Lesotho** through the Lesotho Highlands Water Project — water is sent through tunnels in the mountains to the Vaal Dam\n- **Groundwater** (water underground) is becoming more important as surface water becomes scarce\n- Some coastal cities are building **desalination plants** — these remove salt from sea water to make fresh water'),
  fb(8, 'The Tugela Falls is the second-highest waterfall in the world at ___ metres. South Africa gets extra water from the country of ___.',
    ['948', 'Lesotho'],
    'The Tugela Falls in the Drakensberg mountains is an incredible 948 metres tall — almost a kilometre! South Africa gets water from Lesotho through the Lesotho Highlands Water Project, which uses tunnels and dams to send water across the mountains to the Vaal River system.'),
  q(9, 'Why does Gauteng have to be very careful about water use?',
    ['Because millions of people live there but it depends on water from the Vaal Dam and other transfers', 'Because it never rains in Gauteng', 'Because Gauteng is in a desert', 'Because Gauteng has too many rivers'], 0,
    'Gauteng is South Africa\'s smallest province by area but has the most people — over 15 million! All these people need water for homes, businesses, and industry. Gauteng depends heavily on water from the Vaal Dam and water transferred from Lesotho. If these sources run low during droughts, millions of people are affected.',
    ['Think about how many people live in Gauteng and where their water comes from.']),
  t(10, '## Be a Water Hero!\n\nWater is life. Without it, nothing on Earth can survive. In a dry country like South Africa, every person needs to be a **water hero** — someone who uses water wisely and helps others do the same.\n\n### Your Water-Saving Promise\n\nThink about what you can promise to do:\n- "I will turn off the tap while brushing my teeth"\n- "I will take short showers"\n- "I will tell an adult if I see a leaking tap or pipe"\n- "I will teach my family about saving water"\n\n### What You Have Learned\n\n- Water comes from rain and moves through the **water cycle**\n- South Africa is a **dry country** with limited water\n- Water reaches our taps through **dams, treatment plants, and pipes**\n- Different parts of South Africa have different **rainfall patterns**\n- We must **save water** because it is a precious resource\n- The Cape Town water crisis showed what can happen when water runs low\n\n### Remember\n\nThe water that comes from your tap has travelled a long journey — from the sky, to a river or dam, through a treatment plant, and through kilometres of pipes. It is precious, and we must never waste it!\n\n**Every drop counts. Be a water hero!**'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 7 (Geography – Term 4): Food and Farming
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Where Does Our Food Come From? ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Where Does Our Food Come From?\n\nEvery time you eat a meal, the food on your plate came from somewhere. Most of the food we eat comes from **farms** — places where plants are grown and animals are raised.\n\n### Types of Food from Farms\n\n| Type | Examples |\n|------|----------|\n| **Crops** (plants we grow) | Maize (mielies), wheat, rice, vegetables, fruit |\n| **Livestock** (animals we raise) | Cattle (beef and milk), sheep (mutton and wool), chickens (eggs and meat), pigs (pork) |\n| **Dairy** (from cows and goats) | Milk, cheese, yoghurt, butter |\n\n### Not All Food Comes from Farms\n\nSome food comes from other places:\n- **Fishing** — fish, crayfish, and other seafood from the ocean, rivers, and fish farms\n- **Wild plants** — some people gather wild plants, herbs, and mushrooms\n- **Processed food** — factories turn farm products into bread, cereal, juice, and many other foods\n\nSouth Africa is lucky because our farmers grow **many different types** of food. Our climate is good for farming, and we even sell food to other countries (**export**).'),
  q(2, 'What are crops?',
    ['Plants that are grown on farms for food or other uses', 'Animals that are raised on farms', 'Machines used for farming', 'Buildings where food is stored'], 0,
    'Crops are plants that farmers grow on purpose. The most important crop in South Africa is maize (mielies), which is used to make pap — a staple food for millions of people. Other important crops include wheat, sunflowers, sugarcane, grapes, and many fruits and vegetables.',
    ['Think about what grows in the ground on a farm.']),
  t(3, '## Types of Farming in South Africa\n\nNot all farms are the same. There are different types of farming:\n\n### 1. Crop Farming\n\nGrowing plants for food or other uses:\n- **Maize** — grown mainly in the Free State and North West (the "Maize Triangle")\n- **Wheat** — grown in the Western Cape and Free State\n- **Sugarcane** — grown in KwaZulu-Natal (warm and wet)\n- **Grapes** — grown in the Western Cape (for eating and making wine)\n- **Citrus fruit** — oranges, lemons, naartjies — grown in Limpopo and Eastern Cape\n\n### 2. Livestock Farming\n\nRaising animals:\n- **Cattle** — for beef and milk, mainly in the Free State, Eastern Cape, and KwaZulu-Natal\n- **Sheep** — for wool and meat, mainly in the Eastern Cape and Karoo\n- **Chickens** — across the whole country\n- **Goats** — in drier areas, also for milk and meat\n\n### 3. Mixed Farming\n\nSome farmers do both — they grow crops **and** keep animals. This is called **mixed farming**.'),
  fb(4, 'The area in the Free State and North West where most maize is grown is called the "Maize ___". Sugarcane is mainly grown in ___ because it is warm and wet there.',
    ['Triangle', 'KwaZulu-Natal'],
    'The "Maize Triangle" is a region in the Free State, North West, and Mpumalanga where conditions are ideal for growing maize. Sugarcane needs warm temperatures and plenty of rain, which is why it grows best in KwaZulu-Natal along the coast.'),
  t(5, '## Subsistence Farming vs. Commercial Farming\n\nThere are two main ways that people farm in South Africa:\n\n### Subsistence Farming\n\n**Subsistence farming** is when people grow food for their **own family** to eat. They do not sell it.\n\n- Uses **small pieces of land** (like a garden or small field)\n- Done by hand or with simple tools\n- Grows just enough food for the family\n- Common in **rural areas** of South Africa, especially in the Eastern Cape, KwaZulu-Natal, and Limpopo\n\n### Commercial Farming\n\n**Commercial farming** is when farmers grow food or raise animals to **sell for money**.\n\n- Uses **large farms** with lots of land\n- Uses **machines** like tractors, harvesters, and irrigation systems\n- Produces large amounts of food\n- Food is sold in shops, to factories, or to other countries\n\n| | Subsistence Farming | Commercial Farming |\n|--|--------------------|--------------------|  \n| **Purpose** | Feed the family | Sell for money |\n| **Size** | Small | Large |\n| **Tools** | Simple, by hand | Machines and technology |\n| **Workers** | Family members | Hired workers |\n| **Amount** | Just enough for the family | Large quantities |'),
  q(6, 'What is the difference between subsistence farming and commercial farming?',
    ['Subsistence farming is for the family\'s own food, while commercial farming is to sell food for money', 'Subsistence farming uses big machines, while commercial farming uses hand tools', 'There is no difference — they are the same', 'Subsistence farming is only done in cities'], 0,
    'The key difference is the purpose. Subsistence farmers grow food to feed themselves and their families. Commercial farmers grow food or raise animals to sell, making money from their farming. In South Africa, many people in rural areas practise subsistence farming, while commercial farms produce most of the food sold in shops.',
    ['Think about the word "subsistence" — it means just enough to survive.']),
  t(7, '## From Farm to Table\n\nHave you ever thought about the journey food takes before it reaches your plate? Let us follow a loaf of bread:\n\n### The Journey of Bread\n\n| Step | What Happens |\n|------|-------------|\n| **1. Growing** | A farmer plants wheat seeds in a large field |\n| **2. Harvesting** | When the wheat is ripe (golden), a combine harvester cuts it |\n| **3. Processing** | The wheat grains are taken to a mill and ground into flour |\n| **4. Baking** | A bakery mixes the flour with water, yeast, and salt, then bakes it into bread |\n| **5. Transport** | Trucks deliver the bread to shops and supermarkets |\n| **6. Buying** | Your family buys the bread at the shop |\n| **7. Eating** | You enjoy your sandwich! |\n\n### Think About It\n\nMany people are involved in getting food to your plate:\n- **Farmers** who grow the crops\n- **Truck drivers** who transport the food\n- **Factory workers** who process and package the food\n- **Shop workers** who sell the food\n- **The person at home** who cooks the food\n\nWe should be grateful for all the people who help feed us!'),
  fb(8, 'Wheat is ground into ___ at a mill. The machine that cuts wheat in a field is called a combine ___.',
    ['flour', 'harvester'],
    'After wheat is harvested (cut from the field), it is taken to a mill where the grains are ground into flour. This flour is then used to make bread, pasta, cakes, and many other foods. A combine harvester is a large machine that cuts the wheat, separates the grain from the stalk, and collects it — all in one process.'),
  q(9, 'Which province is known as part of the "Maize Triangle"?',
    ['The Free State — along with North West and parts of Mpumalanga', 'The Western Cape — because it rains a lot there', 'KwaZulu-Natal — because it is warm and wet', 'The Northern Cape — because it has the most farms'], 0,
    'The Maize Triangle covers parts of the Free State, North West, and Mpumalanga provinces. This area has the right combination of rainfall, temperature, and soil for growing maize. Maize (mielies) is South Africa\'s most important crop — it is used to make pap, which is a staple food for millions of South Africans.'),
  t(10, '## Food Gardens\n\nYou do not need a big farm to grow food. Many South Africans grow food in **food gardens** at home, at school, or in their community.\n\n### Why Food Gardens Are Important\n\n- They provide **fresh, healthy food** for the family\n- They help families **save money** on groceries\n- They teach children where food comes from\n- They are good for the **environment**\n- They bring **communities together**\n\n### What You Can Grow\n\nEven a small space can produce food:\n- **Tomatoes** — easy to grow, love sunshine\n- **Spinach** (morogo) — grows quickly, very nutritious\n- **Onions** — useful in almost every meal\n- **Carrots** — grow well in loose soil\n- **Beans** — grow quickly and add nutrients to the soil\n- **Herbs** — parsley, basil, coriander — add flavour to food\n\n### School Food Gardens\n\nMany schools in South Africa have **food gardens** where learners help grow vegetables. These gardens:\n- Teach learners about farming and nature\n- Provide food for the school feeding scheme\n- Give learners responsibility and teamwork skills\n\nMaybe you could start a food garden at home or help improve the one at your school!'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Farming Challenges and Food Security ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## What Is Food Security?\n\n**Food security** means that every person has **enough healthy food** to eat, every day. A country has food security when all its people can afford and access nutritious food.\n\nSadly, many people in South Africa are **food insecure** — they do not always have enough food to eat. This is especially true for:\n- Families living in **poverty**\n- People in **rural areas** far from shops\n- Children who depend on **school feeding** for their main meal\n\n### The Four Pillars of Food Security\n\n| Pillar | What It Means |\n|--------|---------------|\n| **Availability** | Is there enough food in the country? |\n| **Access** | Can people get to the food and afford it? |\n| **Utilisation** | Do people have clean water and fuel to cook the food properly? |\n| **Stability** | Is food available all year round, not just some months? |\n\nAll four pillars must be strong for a country to have food security.'),
  q(2, 'What does "food security" mean?',
    ['Every person has enough healthy food to eat, every day', 'Food is kept safe in a locked room', 'There is a security guard at the supermarket', 'Only some people have enough food'], 0,
    'Food security means all people, at all times, have access to enough safe, nutritious food. In South Africa, food security is a challenge because many families cannot afford to buy enough food, especially healthy food like fruits and vegetables.',
    ['Think about what it means to feel "secure" — safe and not worried.']),
  t(3, '## Challenges Facing Farming in South Africa\n\nFarmers face many difficulties that can affect how much food they produce:\n\n### 1. Drought\n\n**Drought** is when there is very little or no rain for a long time. Without water:\n- Crops die in the fields\n- Animals do not have enough water or grass to eat\n- Farmers lose their income\n- Food prices go up because there is less food available\n\n### 2. Soil Erosion\n\n**Soil erosion** is when the top layer of soil is washed or blown away. This happens because of:\n- Cutting down trees (the roots hold soil in place)\n- Overgrazing (too many animals eating the grass)\n- Ploughing up and down hills instead of across\n\n### 3. Climate Change\n\n**Climate change** is making weather patterns unpredictable:\n- Some areas are getting drier, others are getting wetter\n- Temperatures are rising, which stresses crops\n- Floods and droughts are becoming more common\n\n### 4. Lack of Land\n\nMany subsistence farmers do not have enough land to grow food for their families. During apartheid, Black South Africans were forced onto small, infertile areas.'),
  fb(4, 'When the top layer of soil is washed or blown away, this is called soil ___. A long period without rain is called a ___.',
    ['erosion', 'drought'],
    'Soil erosion is a serious problem because the top layer of soil (topsoil) contains the nutrients that plants need to grow. Once topsoil is lost, it can take hundreds of years to form again. Droughts are devastating for farmers — without rain, crops cannot grow and animals cannot survive.'),
  t(5, '## How South Africa Fights Hunger\n\nThe South African government and other organisations work hard to make sure people have enough food:\n\n### The National School Nutrition Programme (NSNP)\n\nThis is one of the biggest feeding programmes in the world:\n- Over **9 million** school children receive a free meal at school every day\n- The meals include nutritious food like vegetables, samp, beans, and fruit\n- Many children depend on this meal as their **main meal** of the day\n- The programme helps children concentrate and learn better at school\n\n### Social Grants\n\nThe government provides money to help families buy food:\n- **Child Support Grant** — money for families with children\n- **Old Age Pension** — money for elderly people\n- **Disability Grant** — money for people who cannot work\n\n### Food Banks and NGOs\n\nOrganisations like **FoodForward SA** collect extra food from shops and restaurants and give it to people in need. This also reduces food waste.'),
  q(6, 'How many school children in South Africa receive a free meal at school through the NSNP?',
    ['Over 9 million children', 'About 1 million children', 'Only 100 children in Gauteng', 'About 500 000 children'], 0,
    'The National School Nutrition Programme (NSNP) feeds over 9 million learners every school day. It is one of the largest feeding programmes in the world. For many children, especially those from poor families, the school meal is the only proper meal they get in a day.',
    ['Think about how many children are in all the schools across South Africa.']),
  t(7, '## Sustainable Farming\n\n**Sustainable farming** means farming in a way that protects the environment so that people can keep farming in the future. It is about growing food today without damaging the land for tomorrow.\n\n### How Farmers Can Farm Sustainably\n\n| Practice | How It Helps |\n|----------|-------------|\n| **Crop rotation** | Growing different crops each year so the soil stays healthy |\n| **Composting** | Using food scraps and plant waste to make natural fertiliser |\n| **Rainwater harvesting** | Collecting rainwater to use for irrigation |\n| **Drip irrigation** | Dripping water slowly to plant roots — wastes less water than sprinklers |\n| **Indigenous crops** | Growing African crops like sorghum and cowpeas that need less water |\n| **Windbreaks** | Planting rows of trees to stop wind from blowing away topsoil |\n| **Organic farming** | Not using harmful chemicals — better for the soil and for people |\n\n### Indigenous (African) Crops\n\nSome traditional African crops are very good for sustainable farming:\n- **Sorghum** — needs less water than maize\n- **Cowpeas** — add nutrients to the soil\n- **Amadumbe** (taro) — a nutritious root vegetable\n- **Morogo** (wild spinach) — grows easily and is very healthy'),
  fb(8, 'Growing different crops each year to keep the soil healthy is called crop ___. ___ irrigation drips water slowly to plant roots, wasting less water.',
    ['rotation', 'Drip'],
    'Crop rotation means planting different crops in the same field each year. For example, maize one year and beans the next. This keeps the soil healthy because different plants use and add different nutrients. Drip irrigation delivers water directly to the roots of plants through small tubes, using much less water than sprinklers or flooding.'),
  q(9, 'Why are indigenous crops like sorghum good for sustainable farming?',
    ['They need less water and are adapted to local conditions', 'They are the most expensive crops', 'They grow only in greenhouses', 'They need the most fertiliser'], 0,
    'Indigenous (local African) crops like sorghum, cowpeas, and amadumbe have been growing in Africa for thousands of years. They are naturally adapted to local conditions — they need less water, can survive in poorer soil, and are resistant to local pests. This makes them ideal for sustainable farming in South Africa.',
    ['Think about plants that have been growing naturally in Africa for a very long time.']),
  t(10, '## What You Can Do\n\nEven as a Grade 4 learner, you can help with food security and sustainable farming:\n\n### Actions You Can Take\n\n- **Do not waste food** — eat what you are given, ask for smaller portions if you are not hungry\n- **Start a garden** — even a small pot on a windowsill can grow herbs or tomatoes\n- **Compost** — put food scraps (fruit peels, vegetable cuttings) in a compost bin instead of the rubbish\n- **Eat local** — food grown nearby does not need to be transported far\n- **Learn about farming** — visit a farm if you can, or watch videos about how food is grown\n- **Be grateful** — thank the people who grow, prepare, and cook your food\n\n### Remember\n\n- Food does not just appear in shops — it takes **hard work** from farmers and many other people\n- South Africa faces challenges like **drought, poverty, and soil erosion** that threaten our food supply\n- **Sustainable farming** protects the land for future generations\n- **Food security** is a right — every person deserves to have enough healthy food\n- **You** can make a difference, even with small actions like not wasting food and growing a garden\n\nEvery meal is a gift. Use it wisely!'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 8 (Geography – Term 4): Transport
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Types of Transport ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Is Transport?\n\n**Transport** is how people and goods move from one place to another. We use transport every day — to get to school, to deliver food to shops, and to travel to other places.\n\n### The Four Types of Transport\n\n| Type | How It Moves | Examples |\n|------|-------------|----------|\n| **Road transport** | On roads and highways | Cars, buses, taxis, trucks, bicycles, motorbikes |\n| **Rail transport** | On railway tracks | Trains, the Gautrain, metro rail |\n| **Air transport** | Through the sky | Aeroplanes, helicopters |\n| **Water transport** | On water (rivers, lakes, ocean) | Ships, boats, ferries |\n\n### Why Is Transport Important?\n\n- People need to get to **work and school**\n- **Food and goods** need to get to shops\n- People need to **visit family** in other places\n- Transport connects **towns, cities, and countries**\n- **Emergency vehicles** (ambulances, fire engines) save lives'),
  q(2, 'What are the four types of transport?',
    ['Road, rail, air, and water transport', 'Cars, trucks, buses, and bicycles', 'Walking, running, jumping, and swimming', 'Fast, slow, medium, and very fast'], 0,
    'The four main types of transport are road (vehicles on roads), rail (trains on tracks), air (aeroplanes and helicopters in the sky), and water (ships and boats on rivers and oceans). Each type is useful for different purposes.',
    ['Think about the different surfaces or spaces that vehicles move on.']),
  t(3, '## Transport in South Africa\n\nSouth Africa has many different types of transport. Let us look at the most common ones:\n\n### Minibus Taxis\n\nThe **minibus taxi** is the most popular form of transport in South Africa:\n- Over **15 million** South Africans use taxis every day\n- They are affordable and go to many places\n- They run on fixed routes but do not have set timetables\n- Taxi hand signals tell the driver where you want to go\n\n### Buses\n\n- City buses like **MyCiTi** (Cape Town), **Rea Vaya** (Johannesburg), and **Go Durban**\n- Long-distance buses like **Intercape** and **Greyhound** connect cities\n- School buses take children to and from school\n\n### Trains\n\n- **Metrorail** — commuter trains in Cape Town, Johannesburg, Pretoria, and Durban\n- **The Gautrain** — a fast modern train connecting Johannesburg, Pretoria, and OR Tambo Airport\n- **Shosholoza Meyl** — long-distance trains across the country\n- **The Blue Train** — a luxury train famous around the world'),
  fb(4, 'The most popular form of transport in South Africa is the minibus ___. The fast modern train in Gauteng is called the ___.',
    ['taxi', 'Gautrain'],
    'Minibus taxis are the backbone of public transport in South Africa. Over 15 million people ride in taxis every day to get to work, school, and other places. The Gautrain is a modern rapid rail system that opened in 2010 (in time for the FIFA World Cup) and connects Johannesburg, Pretoria, and OR Tambo International Airport.'),
  t(5, '## More Types of Transport in South Africa\n\n### Cars and Private Vehicles\n\n- Many families use a **car** to get around\n- Cars give you freedom to go wherever you want, whenever you want\n- But cars cause **traffic jams** and **air pollution**\n- Petrol and car maintenance are **expensive**\n\n### Aeroplanes\n\n- South Africa has many **airports** — the biggest is OR Tambo International Airport in Johannesburg\n- Airlines like **South African Airways** and **FlySafair** fly between South African cities\n- Aeroplanes are the fastest way to travel long distances\n- International flights connect South Africa to the rest of the world\n\n### Water Transport\n\n- **Ships** carry goods (like cars, clothing, and electronics) to and from South Africa\n- Major ports include **Durban** (the busiest), **Cape Town**, **Gqeberha** (Port Elizabeth), and **Richards Bay**\n- **Fishing boats** catch fish along our coastline\n- **Ferries** carry people and cars — like the Robben Island ferry\n\n### Walking and Cycling\n\n- Many South Africans **walk** to school, work, and the shops\n- **Bicycles** are cheap, healthy, and do not pollute\n- Some cities are building **bicycle lanes** to make cycling safer'),
  q(6, 'Which is the busiest port in South Africa?',
    ['Durban — it handles the most cargo of all South African ports', 'Cape Town — because it is the oldest port', 'Johannesburg — because it is the biggest city', 'Gqeberha — because it is on the east coast'], 0,
    'Durban is South Africa\'s busiest port. It is the largest port in Africa and handles millions of tonnes of cargo every year. Ships bring in goods like electronics, clothing, and machinery, and take out goods like coal, cars, and fruit. Interestingly, Johannesburg does not have a port because it is inland — far from the sea!',
    ['Think about which major South African city is on the coast and near many factories.']),
  t(7, '## Transport Problems in South Africa\n\nSouth Africa\'s transport system has some big challenges:\n\n### Traffic Congestion\n\nToo many cars on the road cause **traffic jams**, especially in big cities:\n- Johannesburg and Cape Town have some of the worst traffic in Africa\n- People spend hours sitting in traffic instead of being with their families\n- Solutions: using public transport, carpooling (sharing rides), and cycling\n\n### Safety\n\nSouth Africa has high numbers of road accidents:\n- Over **14,000 people** die in road accidents every year\n- Causes include speeding, drunk driving, and bad road conditions\n- **Always wear a seatbelt** and use pedestrian crossings\n\n### Affordability\n\n- Transport is **expensive** for many families\n- Some people spend a large part of their income on taxi or bus fares\n- This is especially hard for people who live far from their work\n\n### The State of Rail\n\n- Many Metrorail trains have been **vandalised** (damaged) or stolen\n- Train services in some areas have stopped or are unreliable\n- This forces people to use more expensive taxis and buses'),
  fb(8, 'Over ___ people die in road accidents in South Africa every year. Two causes of road accidents are speeding and ___ driving.',
    ['14,000', 'drunk'],
    'South Africa has one of the highest rates of road deaths in the world. Over 14,000 people are killed on our roads every year. The main causes include speeding (driving too fast), drunk driving (driving after drinking alcohol), and not wearing seatbelts. Road safety is everyone\'s responsibility.'),
  q(9, 'What is one way to reduce traffic jams in cities?',
    ['Using public transport like buses and trains instead of driving alone in a car', 'Building more roads for cars only', 'Buying bigger cars', 'Making everyone stay at home'], 0,
    'Public transport is one of the best solutions to traffic congestion. One bus can carry 50-80 people, replacing 50-80 cars on the road. Systems like the Gautrain, Rea Vaya, and MyCiTi help reduce the number of cars and therefore the traffic. Carpooling (sharing a ride with others) also helps.',
    ['Think about how many cars one bus could replace.']),
  t(10, '## The Future of Transport\n\nTransport is always changing and improving. Here are some exciting developments:\n\n### Electric Vehicles (EVs)\n\n- Cars that run on **electricity** instead of petrol\n- They are better for the environment because they do not produce exhaust fumes\n- Charging stations are being built across South Africa\n\n### Better Public Transport\n\n- More **bus rapid transit** (BRT) systems like Rea Vaya and MyCiTi\n- Plans to fix and expand the **rail network**\n- Better and safer **taxi services**\n\n### Non-Motorised Transport\n\n- More **bicycle lanes** in cities\n- Safer **pedestrian paths** and crossings\n- Encouraging people to walk and cycle for short trips\n\n### What You Can Do\n\n- **Walk or cycle** for short trips — it is healthy and free!\n- **Use public transport** when you can\n- **Always be safe** — wear a seatbelt, use pedestrian crossings, look both ways before crossing the road\n- **Be kind** to other road users — pedestrians, cyclists, and drivers should all share the road\n\nTransport connects people and places. By using transport wisely and safely, we help make South Africa a better place for everyone.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: How Transport Has Changed Over Time ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Transport Long Ago\n\nTransport has changed enormously over time. Let us look at how people moved around in the past:\n\n### The Earliest Transport — Walking\n\nFor most of human history, people could only **walk** to get from one place to another. The San people of southern Africa walked great distances across the veld, following animals and finding water.\n\n### Animals as Transport\n\nThen people learned to use **animals** for transport:\n\n| Animal | How It Was Used |\n|--------|----------------|\n| **Horses** | Riding, pulling carts and wagons |\n| **Donkeys** | Carrying loads, pulling carts |\n| **Oxen** (cattle) | Pulling heavy wagons and ploughs |\n| **Camels** | Carrying loads in desert areas |\n\n### The Ox Wagon\n\nThe **ox wagon** was very important in South Africa\'s history:\n- Used by the **Voortrekkers** (Boer pioneers) when they moved inland from the Cape in the 1830s\n- Pulled by a **span of oxen** (usually 14-16 oxen)\n- Could carry a family\'s belongings, food, and tools\n- Very **slow** — they moved about 15-25 km per day\n- The journey from Cape Town to Johannesburg would have taken **months**!'),
  q(2, 'How did the Voortrekkers travel when they moved inland from the Cape?',
    ['In ox wagons pulled by teams of oxen', 'In cars on highways', 'On aeroplanes', 'On fast trains'], 0,
    'The Voortrekkers used ox wagons to travel. These large wagons were pulled by teams of 14-16 oxen and could carry a family and all their belongings. They were very slow — about 15-25 km per day — so the Great Trek (the journey inland) took many months.',
    ['Think about what type of transport was available in the 1830s — there were no cars, trains, or planes!']),
  t(3, '## The Transport Revolution\n\nIn the 1800s, new inventions changed transport forever:\n\n### The Steam Engine and Railways\n\nThe **steam engine** was invented in Britain and changed the world:\n- **Steam trains** could carry many people and heavy goods much faster than ox wagons\n- South Africa\'s first railway was built in **1860** in Durban\n- Railways connected the ports (Cape Town, Durban) to the mines (Kimberley, Johannesburg)\n- The discovery of diamonds and gold made railways essential for transporting minerals\n\n### The Motor Car\n\nThe first **motor cars** arrived in South Africa around **1897**:\n- At first, only very rich people could afford them\n- Roads were dirt tracks — muddy in rain, dusty in dry weather\n- Over time, cars became cheaper and roads improved\n- Today, there are over **12 million** registered vehicles in South Africa\n\n### Aeroplanes\n\nThe first powered flight was in **1903** by the Wright Brothers in America. In South Africa:\n- The first flights happened in the **1910s**\n- **South African Airways (SAA)** was started in **1934**\n- Today, you can fly from Johannesburg to Cape Town in about **2 hours** — a journey that once took months by ox wagon!'),
  fb(4, 'South Africa\'s first railway was built in ___ in the city of Durban. The first motor cars arrived in South Africa around ___.',
    ['1860', '1897'],
    'The first railway in South Africa was a short line built in Durban in 1860. Railways transformed the country — especially after diamonds (1867) and gold (1886) were discovered, because trains were needed to carry the minerals to the ports. The first cars arrived in South Africa in the late 1890s, but they were very expensive and unreliable.'),
  t(5, '## Transport Timeline\n\nLet us see how transport has changed over time:\n\n| When | Type of Transport |\n|------|-------------------|\n| **Thousands of years ago** | Walking — the only way to travel |\n| **About 5,000 years ago** | Horses and donkeys used for transport |\n| **1830s** | Ox wagons used by Voortrekkers in South Africa |\n| **1860** | First railway built in South Africa (Durban) |\n| **1886** | Railways expanded rapidly after gold discovered |\n| **1897** | First motor cars arrive in South Africa |\n| **1910s** | First flights in South Africa |\n| **1934** | South African Airways (SAA) founded |\n| **1970s-1980s** | Minibus taxis become popular in South Africa |\n| **2010** | Gautrain opened (in time for the FIFA World Cup) |\n| **2020s** | Electric vehicles and better public transport |\n\nIn just 200 years, we went from ox wagons travelling 25 km per day to aeroplanes travelling **900 km per hour**!'),
  q(6, 'Approximately how long does it take to fly from Johannesburg to Cape Town today?',
    ['About 2 hours', 'About 2 days', 'About 2 weeks', 'About 2 months'], 0,
    'A flight from Johannesburg to Cape Town takes about 2 hours. This same journey by ox wagon in the 1800s would have taken several months! By car today it takes about 12-14 hours. The aeroplane has made long-distance travel incredibly fast.',
    ['Think about the fastest type of transport we have.']),
  t(7, '## How Transport Changed South Africa\n\nTransport has shaped South Africa\'s history in important ways:\n\n### Mining and Railways\n\n- The discovery of **diamonds** (1867) and **gold** (1886) created a huge need for railways\n- Railways carried miners to the mines and minerals to the ports\n- Towns grew up along the railway lines\n- Johannesburg grew from a mining camp to a huge city partly because of the railway\n\n### Migrant Labour\n\n- During apartheid, Black South Africans were often forced to live far from their workplaces\n- **Trains** carried millions of workers from townships to cities every day\n- Some workers came from other countries (like Mozambique and Lesotho) to work in the mines\n- The journey was often long and difficult\n\n### Minibus Taxis\n\n- In the 1970s and 1980s, the minibus taxi industry grew rapidly\n- Taxis filled the gap where government transport services did not reach\n- Today, taxis carry more passengers than any other form of public transport in South Africa\n- The taxi industry provides jobs for hundreds of thousands of people'),
  fb(8, 'The discovery of diamonds and ___ created a big need for railways in South Africa. Minibus taxis carry more ___ than any other form of public transport.',
    ['gold', 'passengers'],
    'The mineral revolution transformed South Africa\'s transport system. Railways were rapidly built to connect the diamond fields of Kimberley and the gold mines of the Witwatersrand to the ports. Today, minibus taxis are the backbone of public transport, carrying over 15 million passengers daily across the country.'),
  q(9, 'When was the Gautrain opened?',
    ['2010 — in time for the FIFA World Cup', '1994 — when South Africa became a democracy', '1860 — when the first railway was built', '2020 — during the COVID pandemic'], 0,
    'The Gautrain was opened in 2010, just in time for the FIFA World Cup which South Africa hosted. It is a modern rapid rail system that connects Johannesburg, Pretoria, and OR Tambo International Airport. The Gautrain can travel at speeds of up to 160 km/h.',
    ['Think about a big sporting event that South Africa hosted.']),
  t(10, '## Transport and You\n\n### How Do You Get to School?\n\nThink about how you get to school every day. Do you:\n- **Walk** — This is the most common way for South African children. It is free and healthy, but can be unsafe in some areas.\n- **Ride in a car** — This is comfortable but adds to traffic and pollution.\n- **Take a taxi or bus** — This is a form of public transport.\n- **Ride a bicycle** — This is healthy and environmentally friendly.\n- **Take a school bus** — Some schools provide bus services.\n\n### Making Transport Better\n\nWe can all help make transport better in South Africa:\n\n| Action | How It Helps |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Walk or cycle** for short trips | Less pollution, better health |\n| **Use public transport** | Fewer cars, less traffic |\n| **Carpool** (share rides) | Fewer cars on the road |\n| **Be safe** | Always wear seatbelts, use crossings |\n| **Be patient** | Road rage and rushing cause accidents |\n\n### What You Have Learned\n\n- Transport has changed enormously — from walking to aeroplanes\n- South Africa uses many types of transport — taxis, buses, trains, cars, and planes\n- Transport has challenges — traffic, accidents, cost, and pollution\n- The future holds exciting changes — electric vehicles, better public transport, and safer roads\n\nTransport connects us to the people and places we care about. Whether you walk, ride a taxi, or take the Gautrain — every journey matters!'),
];

